// New arrivals: books bought in for the rooms that were short of them, each with a librarian's note.
//
//   secret     the west-wing bookcase that swings aside (four full columns, all about secrets and hidden rooms)
//   shelves    the general bookcases, in the places that used to repeat a dozen books to fill their gaps
//   evening-*  the Evening Room: quick (under 20 minutes), hour (up to an hour), evening (one to two hours)
//   learners-* the English Reading Room: 1 gentle, 2 steady, 3 richer, 4 challenging, short (the table)
//   signal     the reading room at the Signal House (the night railway)
//   tide       the reading room at Tidebound Quay (the night railway)
//   mars       the Reading Room of Helium, on Mars (mars.js)
//   periodicals the Periodicals Room, beneath the clock in the Grand Hall (periodicals-room.js)
//   irish-*    the Irish Room (irish-room.js): myth (myth and legend), revival (the Revival), writers (Irish writers),
//              gaeilge (books in Irish, checked against the text's Language line)
//   antipodes  the hall at the Antipodes, through the Earth from the Grand Hall's globe (antipodes.js): the way here
//   australian the Australian Room at the Antipodes
//   new-zealand the New Zealand Room at the Antipodes
//   african-*  the African Reading Room, by balloon from the roof garden (african-room.js): ancient (ancient Africa),
//              voices (Africans in their own words), tales (stories)
//   map-*      the Map Room, off the west wing (map-room.js): voyages (voyages of discovery), makers (maps and their
//              makers), lands (lands not on any map)
//   set-texts  the Set Texts Room, through the English Reading Room (set-texts-room.js): plays set for GCSE English
//              Literature that the library did not yet have
//   contested  The Unwelcome Spines, through the door bound in banned pages beyond the Restricted Catalogue (game.js):
//              books once banned, burned, prosecuted or suppressed, that no other shelf holds
//   kipling    the Kipling bookcase in the Grand Hall's south-west corner (kipling-shelf.js): the books of Rudyard
//              Kipling's the library did not yet have, in the order he published them
//   lost-property the Lost Property Office, down the book lift in the west wing (book-lift.js): books no other shelf
//              holds, of every kind, a handful of which are brought out at random on each visit
//   (the International Wing's rooms, spanish, portuguese, french, latin and chinese, are in data/new-books-wing.js,
//   which the library loads only when a reader goes that way)
//
// Each entry is [Gutenberg number or null, title, author, category, room, note]. The numbers are only a first
// guess: scripts/new-books.mjs (run by .github/workflows/new-books.yml) checks every one against the text's own
// title and author, looks up the ones that are wrong or missing, bundles the texts into texts/bundled-gzip and
// counts their words. It writes what it found to data/new-books-resolved.js, and only books found there appear.
window.ATHENAEUM_NEW_BOOKS=[
  // ---------- the secret bookcase: secrets, disguises and rooms that should not exist ----------
  [155,'The Moonstone','Wilkie Collins','Mystery','secret','A cursed Indian diamond vanishes from a Yorkshire country house on the night of a birthday party, and the household tells the story in turns, each witness a little less reliable than the last. T. S. Eliot called it the first, the longest and the best of modern English detective novels. Sergeant Cuff and his roses are worth the journey alone.'],
  [583,'The Woman in White','Wilkie Collins','Mystery','secret','A drawing master walking home across Hampstead Heath at midnight meets a young woman dressed all in white who has escaped from an asylum. What follows is a conspiracy of swapped identities and forged records, and one of fiction’s great villains, Count Fosco, with his white mice. Collins tells it in the voices of many witnesses, as if assembling evidence for a trial.'],
  [558,'The Thirty-Nine Steps','John Buchan','Adventure','secret','Richard Hannay is bored in London until a stranger dies in his flat, leaving a notebook full of code and a plot against the country. He spends the rest of the book running across the Scottish moors with half the spies in Europe behind him. Buchan called it a shocker; it is also the model for nearly every man-on-the-run thriller since.'],
  [2360,'The Riddle of the Sands','Erskine Childers','Sea','secret','Two young Englishmen go duck-shooting in a small yacht among the sandbanks of the Frisian coast and find the Germans rehearsing something they should not. Published in 1903, it was read as a warning and taken seriously in Whitehall. The fog, the tides and the charts are as much a part of the plot as the spies.'],
  [95,'The Prisoner of Zenda','Anthony Hope','Adventure','secret','An idle Englishman on holiday in the little kingdom of Ruritania discovers he is the exact double of its king, just as the king is drugged on the eve of his coronation. Duty, disguise and a doomed romance follow, at the point of a sword. It invented a whole country, and gave the language a word for imaginary little kingdoms.'],
  [8954,'Lady Audley’s Secret','Mary Elizabeth Braddon','Gothic','secret','A golden-haired governess marries a wealthy baronet, and his nephew begins to notice that a friend vanished while visiting the house. Braddon’s sensation novel was a scandalous bestseller in 1862. The secret is less shocking now than the question of why the heroine felt she had to keep it.'],
  [60,'The Scarlet Pimpernel','Baroness Orczy','Adventure','secret','During the Terror, a band of Englishmen rescues French aristocrats from the guillotine, led by a man known only by the small red flower he signs his notes with. At home in London, Sir Percy Blakeney appears to be the dullest fop in society. The hero with a foolish public face begins, more or less, here.'],
  [434,'The Circular Staircase','Mary Roberts Rinehart','Mystery','secret','A sensible spinster rents a country house for the summer, and on the first weekend a man is shot dead at the foot of its spiral staircase. Rinehart’s heroine keeps wondering what she might have prevented, which gave rise to the “had I but known” school of mysteries. There is a hidden room, of course; this shelf would not have it otherwise.'],
  [1872,'The Red House Mystery','A. A. Milne','Mystery','secret','Before Winnie-the-Pooh, A. A. Milne wrote one detective novel: a brother back from Australia, a shot behind a locked office door, and two young friends playing Holmes and Watson in a country house. It is light, clever and cheerfully aware of its own genre. There is also a secret passage, which is why it lives here.'],
  [4047,'The Leavenworth Case','Anna Katharine Green','Mystery','secret','A wealthy New York merchant is found shot in his library, and suspicion falls on one of his two beautiful nieces. Published in 1878, nine years before Sherlock Holmes, it was one of the first detective novels written by a woman, and a huge bestseller. Agatha Christie read it as a girl and never forgot it.'],
  [null,'The Big Bow Mystery','Israel Zangwill','Mystery','secret','A man is found with his throat cut in a bedroom locked and bolted from the inside, in a lodging house in the East End of London. Zangwill’s short novel of 1891 is often called the first full-length locked-room mystery. The solution is outrageous, and entirely fair.'],
  [2759,'The Man in the Iron Mask','Alexandre Dumas','Adventure','secret','The ageing musketeers discover the most dangerous secret in France: a prisoner in the Bastille whose face must never be seen, because it is the face of the king. Dumas built the story on a real prisoner of Louis XIV whose identity is still argued over. It is the last part of the musketeers’ long saga, and the saddest.'],
  [null,'Trent’s Last Case','E. C. Bentley','Mystery','secret','An American financier is found shot in the garden of his English country house, and the painter and amateur detective Philip Trent solves it with dazzling logic. Bentley wrote it in 1913 partly to show that great detectives could be fallible. Say no more; the title is a clue.'],
  [974,'The Secret Agent','Joseph Conrad','Conscience','secret','Mr Verloc keeps a shabby shop in Soho, spies for a foreign embassy, and is ordered to blow up the Greenwich Observatory. Conrad drew on a real bombing of 1894 and told it in a voice of cold irony. It is the quietest and most devastating book on this shelf, and much of it is about the people nobody tells the secret to.'],
  [1685,'The Mystery of the Yellow Room','Gaston Leroux','Mystery','secret','A scream in the night, a shot, and a young woman found injured in a room locked from the inside, with her attacker nowhere to be found. Before The Phantom of the Opera, Leroux wrote this classic locked-room puzzle, solved by an eighteen-year-old reporter. The floor plans are part of the fun.'],
  [14851,'Uncle Silas','J. Sheridan Le Fanu','Gothic','secret','When her father dies, young Maud Ruthyn is sent to live with her uncle Silas, a man once suspected of murder, in his decaying house in Derbyshire. Le Fanu turns the terms of a will into a slow-closing trap. Madame de la Rougierre, the governess, is one of the most unsettling figures in Victorian fiction.'],

  // ---------- the African Reading Room (by balloon from the roof garden, african-room.js): ancient Africa ----------
  [1666,'The Golden Asse','Apuleius','Wonder','african-ancient','Lucius, too curious about magic, rubs on the wrong ointment and is turned into an ass, and goes through a long run of misadventures before the goddess Isis restores him. Apuleius was born at Madauros in Numidia, in what is now Algeria, in the second century, and his Metamorphoses is the only Latin novel to survive complete. This is William Adlington’s translation of 1566, with the story of Cupid and Psyche in the middle of it.'],
  [26294,'The Apologia and Florida of Apuleius of Madaura','Apuleius','Philosophy','african-ancient','In about 158 Apuleius stood trial at Sabratha, in what is now Libya, accused of winning his rich wife by magic, and this is the speech he made in his own defence. It is clever, learned, vain and very funny about his accusers. The Florida are short passages from his other speeches, gathered later.'],
  [22188,'The Comedies of Terence','Terence','Drama','african-ancient','Publius Terentius Afer came to Rome from Carthage as a slave, was freed by his master, and wrote six comedies in the 160s BC, all of which survive. His surname, Afer, means the African. One of his lines is still quoted: I am human, and I think nothing human alien to me.'],
  [7386,'Egyptian Tales, Translated from the Papyri','W. M. Flinders Petrie','Legend','african-ancient','Stories from Egyptian papyri of the Old and Middle Kingdoms, translated by the archaeologist Flinders Petrie in 1895. Among them are the tale of Sinuhe, an official who flees Egypt and longs all his life to be buried there, and stories of magicians told at the court of King Khufu. They are among the oldest stories in the world.'],
  [7145,'The Book of the Dead','E. A. Wallis Budge','Legend','african-ancient','The Egyptian spells that guided the dead through the dangers of the underworld to the judgment, where the heart was weighed against the feather of truth. E. A. Wallis Budge of the British Museum translated them from the Papyrus of Ani, which was made more than three thousand years ago. His readings have dated, but the spells are as strange and beautiful as ever.'],
  [15932,'The Literature of the Ancient Egyptians','E. A. Wallis Budge','History','african-ancient','A survey from 1914 of what the Egyptians wrote: hymns, tales, wisdom, love songs and letters, with long passages in translation. It is a good map of a literature more than four thousand years old. Some of Budge’s views have dated, but the texts he chose have not.'],

  // ---------- the African Reading Room: Africans in their own words ----------
  [15399,'The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano','Olaudah Equiano','History','african-voices','Olaudah Equiano’s autobiography, published in London in 1789, tells of being taken from Igboland as a child, of slavery in the Americas and at sea, and of buying his own freedom. It became a bestseller and one of the key books of the campaign against the slave trade. Some historians have questioned where he was born; no one has questioned the force of the book.'],
  [66908,'Letters of the Late Ignatius Sancho, an African','Ignatius Sancho','Society','african-voices','Ignatius Sancho was born on a slave ship, grew up in London and became a shopkeeper, composer and man of letters, the first African known to have voted in a British election. His letters, published in 1782, two years after his death, are warm, witty and full of Laurence Sterne, with whom he corresponded. They were among the first writing by an African to be widely read in Britain.'],
  [409,'Poems on Various Subjects, Religious and Moral','Phillis Wheatley','Poetry','african-voices','Phillis Wheatley was taken from West Africa as a child, enslaved in Boston, and published this book of poems in London in 1773, when she was about twenty. It was the first book of poems by an African American woman. It had to carry a statement signed by eighteen Boston men that she had written it herself.'],
  [1452,'Native Life in South Africa','Sol T. Plaatje','History','african-voices','Sol Plaatje’s account of the Natives’ Land Act of 1913, which barred black South Africans from owning or renting most of the land of their own country, and of the evicted families he met on the roads. Plaatje was a journalist, translator and a founder of what became the African National Congress, and he wrote the book in London while campaigning against the Act. Its first sentence is one of the most quoted in South African writing.'],
  [22631,'An Account of Timbuctoo and Housa Territories in the Interior of Africa','Abd Salam Shabeeny','Journey','african-voices','Abd Salam Shabeeny, a merchant from Tetuan in Morocco, crossed the Sahara as a young man and lived for a time in Timbuktu and the Hausa country. Years later he described what he had seen to James Grey Jackson, an English merchant in Morocco, who published it in 1820. It is one of the few accounts of Timbuktu in those years by a traveller from Africa itself.'],
  [1441,'The Story of an African Farm','Olive Schreiner','Society','african-voices','Two cousins, Lyndall and Em, and a farmer’s son, Waldo, grow up on a lonely farm in the Karoo. Olive Schreiner, born at a mission station in the Cape, published it in 1883 under the name Ralph Iron, and its heroine’s arguments for women’s independence made it famous. It is often called the first great South African novel.'],

  // ---------- the African Reading Room: stories ----------
  [37472,'Zanzibar Tales','George W. Bateman','Legend','african-tales','Animal stories and wonder tales told on the east coast of Africa, translated from Swahili by George W. Bateman and published in 1901. The hare, the tortoise and the monkey outwit stronger creatures, as small animals do in stories across the continent. Verne’s balloon set off from Zanzibar too.'],
  [66923,'West African Folk-Tales','W. H. Barker','Legend','african-tales','Stories from the Gold Coast, now Ghana, many of them about Anansi the spider, the Akan trickster to whom, it is said, all stories belong. W. H. Barker and Cecilia Sinclair gathered them and published them in 1917. Anansi’s stories crossed the Atlantic and are still told in the Caribbean.'],
  [34655,'Folk Stories from Southern Nigeria','Elphinstone Dayrell','Legend','african-tales','Stories from the Cross River country of southern Nigeria, written down by Elphinstone Dayrell, a colonial district officer, and published in 1910 with an introduction by Andrew Lang. There are tortoises, leopards, kings and ghosts, and explanations of how things came to be. They read like stories meant to be told aloud.'],
  [48828,'Cunnie Rabbit, Mr. Spider and the Other Beef','Florence M. Cronise','Legend','african-tales','Animal stories from Sierra Leone, gathered by Florence Cronise and Henry Ward and published in 1903. Cunnie Rabbit is small and clever, and Mr Spider greedy and full of tricks. Readers who know Brer Rabbit will recognise some of the plots, which travelled to America with enslaved Africans.'],
  [58900,'Where Animals Talk','Robert Hamill Nassau','Legend','african-tales','Animal stories from the coast of Gabon and Equatorial Guinea, collected by Robert Hamill Nassau, an American missionary who spent more than forty years in West Africa. The tortoise, the leopard and the little antelope compete, cheat and argue. They were published in 1912.'],
  [10085,'Moorish Literature','René Basset','Legend','african-tales','Ballads, tales of the Berbers and stories of the Kabyles of Algeria, gathered by the French scholar René Basset and published in English in 1901. There are romances of Moorish Spain as well as folk tales from the mountains of North Africa. It is an old anthology, and a good way to meet stories rarely translated into English.'],
  [73413,'Reynard the Fox in South Africa','W. H. I. Bleek','Legend','african-tales','Fables of the Khoekhoe people of southern Africa, translated by Wilhelm Bleek in 1864, with the jackal as their sly hero. Bleek was a German linguist who later, with Lucy Lloyd, recorded thousands of pages of the /Xam language from San storytellers. These stories came first, and they are short, sharp and funny.'],

  // ---------- the Antipodes: the way here (the hall at the far side of the world, antipodes.js) ----------
  [8106,'Captain Cook’s Journal During His First Voyage Round the World','James Cook','Journey','antipodes','James Cook’s own journal of the voyage of the Endeavour from 1768 to 1771, printed from the manuscript and edited by W. J. L. Wharton in 1893. On it Cook charted the coasts of New Zealand and sailed up the east coast of Australia, landing at Botany Bay in April 1770. The entries are plain, exact and seamanlike, and now and then astonished.'],
  [12929,'A Voyage to Terra Australis','Matthew Flinders','Journey','antipodes','Matthew Flinders’s account of his voyage in the Investigator, which in 1802 and 1803 sailed all the way round Australia and showed it to be one continent. He argued for calling it Australia, and the name stuck. He finished the book after six and a half years held by the French on Mauritius, and it was published in London the day before he died, in July 1814.'],
  [944,'The Voyage of the Beagle','Charles Darwin','Journey','antipodes','Darwin’s account of the Beagle’s voyage round the world from 1831 to 1836, the five years that made him a naturalist. Near the end the ship called at New Zealand, Sydney and Tasmania, and he left Australia, he wrote, “without sorrow or regret”. Australians have long since forgiven him, and named a city after him.'],
  [2895,'Following the Equator','Mark Twain','Journey','antipodes','Mark Twain’s account of the lecture tour round the world he made in 1895 and 1896 to pay off his debts. A good part of it is spent in Australia and New Zealand, where he admired the cities and was charmed by the people. It is as funny as you would expect, and angrier than you might, about what the colonists did to the people who were there before them.'],

  // ---------- the Antipodes: the Australian Room ----------
  [3424,'For the Term of His Natural Life','Marcus Clarke','History','australian','Rufus Dawes is transported to Van Diemen’s Land for a murder he did not commit, and the novel follows him through the penal settlements of Macquarie Harbour, Port Arthur and Norfolk Island. Marcus Clarke worked from the colonial records, and published it as a serial in Melbourne between 1870 and 1872. It is the great novel of the convict system, and a harrowing one.'],
  [1198,'Robbery Under Arms','Rolf Boldrewood','Adventure','australian','Dick Marston, in prison under sentence of death, tells how he and his brother fell in with the gentleman bushranger Captain Starlight: cattle-duffing, the goldfields and long rides ahead of the troopers. Rolf Boldrewood was the pen-name of Thomas Alexander Browne, a squatter who became a police magistrate and goldfields commissioner. It came out as a serial in the Sydney Mail in 1882 and 1883, and has hardly been out of print since.'],
  [11620,'My Brilliant Career','Miles Franklin','Society','australian','Sybylla Melvyn, clever, restless and furious at being poor and plain, grows up on a struggling farm in the bush of New South Wales and refuses the life laid out for her. Miles Franklin wrote it in her teens, and it was published in Edinburgh in 1901 with a preface by Henry Lawson. Australia’s best-known literary prize is named after her.'],
  [7144,'While the Billy Boils','Henry Lawson','Modern','australian','Henry Lawson’s first big book of stories, from 1896: shearers, swagmen, selectors and drovers’ dogs, told in a plain, dry voice that is very funny and then suddenly not at all. The billy is the tin can a bushman boiled his tea in over the fire. There is one on the fire in this room.'],
  [1036,'Joe Wilson and His Mates','Henry Lawson','Modern','australian','Joe Wilson tells of his courtship and marriage, and of trying to make a living on a selection in the bush, in four linked stories that are among the best things Lawson wrote. “Water Them Geraniums” is the saddest of them and perhaps the truest. The book was published in 1901, while Lawson was living in London.'],
  [3470,'Such Is Life','Joseph Furphy','Modern','australian','Tom Collins, a minor government official with a taste for philosophy, recounts a few scattered days among the bullock-drivers of the Riverina in 1883, and misses half of what is going on around him. Joseph Furphy described its temper as democratic and its bias as offensively Australian. Published by the Bulletin in 1903, it is long, digressive and funny, and took a long time to find its readers.'],
  [3728,'The Getting of Wisdom','Henry Handel Richardson','Society','australian','Laura Rambotham, a clever and awkward girl from the country, is sent to a ladies’ college in Melbourne and learns, painfully, how to fit in. Henry Handel Richardson was the pen-name of Ethel Florence Lindesay Richardson, who drew on her own schooldays at the Presbyterian Ladies’ College. Published in 1910, it is as sharp about snobbery and small lies as any school story ever written.'],
  [4731,'Seven Little Australians','Ethel Turner','Wonder','australian','The seven unruly children of an army captain live in a rambling house beside the Parramatta River, and drive their stern father and young stepmother to distraction. Ethel Turner was in her early twenties when it was published in 1894, and it has been in print ever since. Readers who have finished it will know why it is not only a funny book.'],
  [3833,'Australian Legendary Tales','K. Langloh Parker','Legend','australian','Katie Langloh Parker lived on a station on the Narran River in New South Wales and wrote down these stories as the Aboriginal people there, the Yuwaalaraay, told them to her. They tell how the birds and animals came by their shapes and their calls, and the stars their places. She wrote them in the English of 1896, and the stories belong to their tellers.'],
  [4730,'The Songs of a Sentimental Bloke','C. J. Dennis','Poetry','australian','Bill, a larrikin from the back streets of Melbourne, falls for Doreen and tells the whole story of their courtship and marriage in verse, in the slang of the street. C. J. Dennis published it in 1915 and it sold in enormous numbers, many copies going to soldiers at the front in a pocket edition. It comes with a glossary, which you will need.'],
  [4699,'We of the Never-Never','Jeannie Gunn','Journey','australian','In 1902 Jeannie Gunn went with her new husband to the Elsey cattle station on the Roper River in the Northern Territory, where the stockmen called her the Little Missus. Her husband died there of malarial dysentery the next year. Her account of that year, published in 1908, is in part a memorial to him.'],
  [4223,'The Mystery of a Hansom Cab','Fergus Hume','Mystery','australian','A man is found dead in a hansom cab on the St Kilda Road in Melbourne, and the passenger who got out on the way has vanished. Fergus Hume, a young barrister’s clerk, published it at his own expense in Melbourne in 1886, a year before the first Sherlock Holmes story, and it became one of the bestselling detective novels of the century. He had sold the rights for fifty pounds, and went on to write well over a hundred more.'],
  [23625,'The Magic Pudding','Norman Lindsay','Wonder','australian','Bunyip Bluegum, a koala of excellent manners, takes to the road and falls in with a sailor, a penguin and Albert, a bad-tempered pudding who can be eaten for ever and always grows back. A possum and a wombat, professional pudding thieves, spend the book trying to steal him. Norman Lindsay wrote and drew it in 1918, the story goes, to settle an argument about whether children would rather read about fairies or food.'],
  [3535,'A Narrative of the Expedition to Botany-Bay','Watkin Tench','History','australian','Watkin Tench, a young officer of marines, sailed with the First Fleet in 1787 and wrote this account of the voyage and the first months of the settlement at Sydney Cove. It was published in London in 1789. He is curious and humane, and his pages on the Aboriginal people he met are among the most valuable we have.'],
  [78420,'Bush Studies','Barbara Baynton','Modern','australian','Six stories of women alone in the bush, published in London in 1902 and as far from the comradeship of Lawson’s mates as it is possible to get. Baynton’s bush is a place of isolation and menace, and her stories are hard to forget. “The Chosen Vessel” is the one most often reprinted.'],

  // ---------- the Antipodes: the New Zealand Room ----------
  [44385,'Bliss, and Other Stories','Katherine Mansfield','Modern','new-zealand','Mansfield’s collection of 1920, with “Prelude”, the long story of the Burnell family’s move to a house in the country outside Wellington, drawn from her own childhood. The title story, of a young wife on the evening of a dinner party, ends on one of the quietest and most devastating discoveries in fiction. Mansfield was born in Wellington in 1888 and left New Zealand for good at nineteen.'],
  [66871,'The Doves’ Nest, and Other Stories','Katherine Mansfield','Modern','new-zealand','The stories Mansfield was working on in the last year of her life, some finished and some fragments, gathered by her husband John Middleton Murry after her death in January 1923. Among them are “The Fly” and “The Doll’s House”, in which the Burnell children are given a doll’s house and the little Kelveys are not allowed to see it. The unfinished pieces show how she built a story.'],
  [1472,'In a German Pension','Katherine Mansfield','Modern','new-zealand','Mansfield’s first book, from 1911: sharp, satirical sketches of the guests at a Bavarian spa where she stayed in 1909, eating, bathing and lecturing one another. She was in her early twenties. She later refused to let it be reprinted, thinking it immature, which is a good reason to read it.'],
  [59276,'Poems','Katherine Mansfield','Poetry','new-zealand','Katherine Mansfield’s poems, gathered by John Middleton Murry and published in 1923, the year she died. Some are about her brother Leslie, killed in France in 1915, whose death turned her back to the New Zealand of their childhood. They are the margin of her work, and the margin is worth reading.'],
  [6104,'Station Life in New Zealand','Lady Barker','Journey','new-zealand','Letters home from Mary Anne Barker, who went out to Canterbury in 1865 with her second husband and lived on a sheep station in the hills above the plains. There are floods, a shearing, a ball, and the great snowstorm of 1867 that buried the sheep. It is cheerful, observant and still a pleasure to read.'],
  [33342,'Old New Zealand','F. E. Maning','History','new-zealand','F. E. Maning came to the Hokianga in 1833, married into a Māori family and lived as a Pākehā Māori, a European who had made his life in the Māori world. This book, published in 1863 under the name “A Pakeha Maori”, looks back on those early days with humour and some exaggeration. It is a teller’s book rather than a historian’s, and a classic.'],
  [3235,'A First Year in Canterbury Settlement','Samuel Butler','Journey','new-zealand','Samuel Butler went out to New Zealand in 1859, took up a sheep run in the high country of Canterbury and wrote home about it, and his father had the letters published in 1863. The mountains and the unexplored passes behind his station turn up again, a few years later, as the way into Erewhon. He came home in 1864 with his money doubled.'],
  [1971,'Erewhon Revisited','Samuel Butler','Adventure','new-zealand','Twenty years after his first visit, the explorer Higgs goes back to Erewhon and finds that his escape by balloon has made him the centre of a new religion, Sunchildism. Butler wrote this sequel in 1901, the year before he died. It is gentler than Erewhon and, in its way, funnier.'],
  [12411,'The Long White Cloud','William Pember Reeves','History','new-zealand','A history of New Zealand by William Pember Reeves, the country’s Minister of Labour in the 1890s and the man behind its pioneering law on industrial arbitration. He wrote it in London in 1898, as New Zealand’s Agent-General there. The title is the usual translation of Aotearoa, the Māori name for the country.'],
  [11933,'A Narrative of a Nine Months’ Residence in New Zealand in 1827','Augustus Earle','Journey','new-zealand','Augustus Earle, a travelling artist, spent nine months in the north of New Zealand in 1827 and 1828, drawing and living among Māori, and wrote this lively, opinionated account of it. He later sailed as the artist on the Beagle with Charles Darwin, until ill health put him ashore. His pictures of New Zealand are among the first by a trained painter.'],
  [45688,'The Adventures of Kimble Bent','James Cowan','History','new-zealand','Kimble Bent, an American-born soldier in a British regiment, deserted in Taranaki in 1865 and went over to the Māori who were fighting it, living with them through the wars that followed. In old age he told his story to James Cowan, who published it in 1911. It is one of the strangest true stories of the New Zealand Wars.'],
  [73766,'Maori Folk-Tales of the Port Hills','James Cowan','Legend','new-zealand','James Cowan, who grew up speaking Māori and spent his life recording the history of the country, gathered these stories of the hills above Lyttelton Harbour, near Christchurch. They are the traditions of the place, of its peaks and headlands and the people who named them. It is a short book, best read with a map of the hills beside it.'],

  // ---------- the Irish Room: myth and legend ----------
  [14465,'Gods and Fighting Men','Lady Gregory','Legend','irish-myth','Lady Gregory’s retelling, from 1904, of the stories of the Tuatha Dé Danann, the old gods of Ireland, and of Finn mac Cumhaill and the Fianna. She wrote it in the English of Kiltartan, the Galway countryside around her house at Coole, with the rhythm of Irish running under it. W. B. Yeats wrote the preface.'],
  [34081,'Myths & Legends of the Celtic Race','T. W. Rolleston','Legend','irish-myth','T. W. Rolleston’s survey of Celtic myth, from the invasions of Ireland to the Ulster heroes, Finn and the Fianna, and the Welsh tales. Rolleston, a founder of the Irish Literary Society, wrote it in 1911 for readers who knew none of it. It is still one of the clearest ways in.'],
  [7885,'Celtic Fairy Tales','Joseph Jacobs','Legend','irish-myth','Joseph Jacobs gathered these twenty-six tales from Irish, Scottish, Welsh, Cornish and Manx sources in 1892 and retold them for children. Most come from Ireland: Connla and the fairy maiden, Deirdre, and Munachar and Manachar. John D. Batten’s drawings are half the pleasure.'],
  [2892,'Irish Fairy Tales','James Stephens','Legend','irish-myth','James Stephens retells ten stories of Finn mac Cumhaill and the Fianna, beginning with Finn’s boyhood, in prose that is funny and strange in equal measure. It was published in 1920 with pictures by Arthur Rackham. Stephens’s The Crock of Gold is on the next shelf.'],
  [5092,'The Coming of Cuculain','Standish O’Grady','Legend','irish-myth','Standish O’Grady’s retelling of the boyhood of Cúchulainn, the Hound of Ulster, published in 1894. His histories of heroic Ireland, written in the 1870s, set the young Yeats and his friends alight, and he is often called the father of the Irish literary revival. This short book is the best place to meet him.'],
  [36540,'Myths and Folk Tales of Ireland','Jeremiah Curtin','Legend','irish-myth','Jeremiah Curtin, an American and the son of Irish emigrants, took these stories down from storytellers in the west of Ireland in the 1880s. There are giants, enchanted princesses, the Fianna and a great deal of cunning. Curtin was a gifted linguist who also translated Sienkiewicz’s Polish novels into English.'],
  [60782,'Beside the Fire','Douglas Hyde','Legend','irish-myth','Douglas Hyde’s collection of Irish folk stories, published in 1890, taken down from storytellers in Connacht, with the Irish texts and his translations. Hyde helped found the Gaelic League in 1893 to keep the language alive, and in 1938 he became the first President of Ireland. His preface is a plea to collect the stories before they were gone.'],
  [61436,'Ancient Legends, Mystic Charms & Superstitions of Ireland','Lady Wilde','Legend','irish-myth','Jane Wilde, who wrote patriotic verse as Speranza and was the mother of Oscar Wilde, drew on her husband William’s notes and on the people of the west for this book of 1887. Fairy forts, the evil eye, cures for every ailment and the stories told about them are all here. It is a rich, strange book to dip into.'],
  [10459,'The Celtic Twilight','W. B. Yeats','Legend','irish-myth','Yeats’s little book of 1893: stories of fairies, ghosts and visions, heard from country people in Sligo and Galway. It gave its name to a whole mood of Irish writing. The pieces are short, some only a page, and are best read by the fire.'],
  [43973,'Visions and Beliefs in the West of Ireland, First Series','Lady Gregory','Legend','irish-myth','Lady Gregory spent years listening to her neighbours around Coole and Kiltartan talk about the other world: the Sidhe, changelings, seers and the dead who come back. She gathered their words and published them in 1920, with essays and notes by Yeats. It is the nearest thing in the library to hearing them speak.'],

  // ---------- the Irish Room: the Revival ----------
  [1240,'The Playboy of the Western World','J. M. Synge','Drama','irish-revival','Christy Mahon arrives at a pub in Mayo claiming to have killed his father, and the whole village falls in love with him for it. When the play opened at the Abbey Theatre in January 1907 the audience rioted, partly over the word “shift”. Synge’s language, taken from the talk he heard in the west, is as rich as anything in English.'],
  [994,'Riders to the Sea','J. M. Synge','Drama','irish-revival','A one-act play of 1904, set on an island off the west of Ireland, where Maurya has already lost her husband and sons to the sea and watches her last son set out for the mainland. Synge wrote it after his summers on the Aran Islands. It runs to barely half an hour on the stage.'],
  [4381,'The Aran Islands','J. M. Synge','Journey','irish-revival','Synge’s account of four summers on the Aran Islands between 1898 and 1901, living with islanders, learning Irish and listening to their stories. Yeats had told him to go there and express a life that had never found expression. Many of the stories he heard turn up later in his plays.'],
  [5168,'The Land of Heart’s Desire','W. B. Yeats','Drama','irish-revival','A short verse play in which a young bride in Sligo is lured away by a fairy child on May Eve. Staged in London in 1894, it was the first of Yeats’s plays to be performed. The fairy child sings “The wind blows out of the gates of the day”.'],
  [32491,'The Wild Swans at Coole','W. B. Yeats','Poetry','irish-revival','Yeats’s poems of 1917 to 1919, named for the swans on the lake at Coole Park, Lady Gregory’s house in Galway. Here are “An Irish Airman Foresees His Death” and the elegy for her son, Robert Gregory. He counts nine-and-fifty swans, nineteen autumns after he first counted them.'],
  [41653,'Seven Short Plays','Lady Gregory','Drama','irish-revival','Seven one-act plays Lady Gregory wrote for the Abbey Theatre, among them Spreading the News, in which a rumour grows into a murder, and The Rising of the Moon, in which a policeman meets a rebel on a quay at night. She founded the theatre with Yeats. The plays are short, funny and very good read aloud.'],
  [1605,'The Crock of Gold','James Stephens','Wonder','irish-revival','Two philosophers in a pine wood, a leprechaun’s stolen pot of gold, a policeman and the god Pan all find their way into the same story, and so, in the end, does Angus Óg. James Stephens published it in 1912, and it won the Polignac Prize. It is comic, wise and entirely its own.'],
  [4034,'The Untilled Field','George Moore','Modern','irish-revival','George Moore’s stories of rural Ireland: priests, emigrants and small farmers, and the young who want to leave. He wrote them to be translated into Irish for the Gaelic League, and they appeared in Irish before this English edition of 1903. It is often counted the first modern Irish book of short stories.'],
  [12871,'The Insurrection in Dublin','James Stephens','History','irish-revival','James Stephens kept a record of Easter Week 1916 as it happened around him in Dublin: the shooting, the rumours, the burning of Sackville Street. It was published within months, before anyone knew how the story would end. It is the Rising seen from the pavement.'],
  [65953,'Our Irish Theatre','Lady Gregory','History','irish-revival','Lady Gregory’s account of how she, Yeats and Edward Martyn founded the Irish Literary Theatre in 1899, and how it became the Abbey. It covers the Playboy riots, and the company’s tour of America in 1911 and 1912, where the play caused riots again. She wrote it in 1913, while it was all still happening.'],

  // ---------- the Irish Room: Irish writers ----------
  [1424,'Castle Rackrent','Maria Edgeworth','Satire','irish-writers','Thady Quirk, the family’s faithful steward, tells how four generations of the Rackrents drank, gambled and sued their way through their estate. Maria Edgeworth published it in 1800, and it is often called the first regional novel in English. Walter Scott said it made him want to do the same for Scotland.'],
  [34630,'Some Experiences of an Irish R.M.','Martin Ross and E. Œ. Somerville','Comedy','irish-writers','Major Yeates, a retired officer, becomes a Resident Magistrate in West Cork and is outwitted at every turn by horse dealers, poachers and his landlord, Flurry Knox. Edith Somerville and her cousin Violet Martin, who wrote as Martin Ross, published the stories in 1899. They are among the funniest in the library.'],
  [59138,'The Real Charlotte','Martin Ross and E. Œ. Somerville','Society','irish-writers','Charlotte Mullen, plain, clever and ruthless, schemes for land and love in a small Irish town. Somerville and Ross published it in 1894, and it is their masterpiece. Many readers think it the finest Irish novel of the nineteenth century.'],
  [2667,'The Vicar of Wakefield','Oliver Goldsmith','Society','irish-writers','Dr Primrose, a country vicar, loses his fortune, his home and very nearly his family, and bears it all with a patience that is sometimes funny and sometimes heartbreaking. Oliver Goldsmith, born in County Longford, wrote it in London. Samuel Johnson sold the manuscript to save him from arrest for his rent.'],
  [16018,'The Black Prophet: A Tale Of Irish Famine','William Carleton','Society','irish-writers','William Carleton’s novel of an earlier Irish famine, published in 1847 as the Great Famine was killing hundreds of thousands. Carleton, the son of Irish-speaking farmers in Tyrone, knew rural poverty from the inside. It is angry, melodramatic and unforgettable.'],

  // ---------- the Irish Room: books in Irish ----------
  [50913,'Niamh','Peter O’Leary','Romance','irish-gaeilge','A novel in Irish by An tAthair Peadar Ua Laoghaire, the Cork priest whose Irish, the speech of Muskerry, became a model for learners. It is set in the time of Brian Boru and the Battle of Clontarf. It was published in 1907, when books in Irish were still rare.'],
  [79587,'Cormac Ua Conaill','Patrick S. Dinneen','History','irish-gaeilge','A historical novel in Irish by An tAthair Pádraig Ua Duinnín, Father Patrick Dinneen, best known for his great Irish-English dictionary. It was published in 1901. It was one of the first novels written in modern Irish.'],
  [78840,'Aoḋ Ó Néill','Pádraig Ó Seaghdha','History','irish-gaeilge','A book in Irish about Aodh Ó Néill, Hugh O’Neill, the Earl of Tyrone who led the Nine Years’ War against Elizabeth I. Its author, Pádraig Ó Seaghdha, wrote as Conán Maol and was one of the busiest writers of the early Gaelic League. It is printed in the Gaelic type, with dotted letters.'],
  [78815,'Maidean i mBéarra agus dánta eile','Osborn Bergin','Poetry','irish-gaeilge','Poems in Irish by Osborn Bergin, the Cork-born scholar of Old and Middle Irish. The title poem is a morning in Béarra, the peninsula in west Cork and Kerry. Scholars knew him for editing the poems of others; these are his own.'],

  // ---------- the general bookcases ----------
  [2814,'Dubliners','James Joyce','Modern','shelves','Fifteen stories of ordinary Dublin lives, beginning in childhood and ending with “The Dead”, one of the finest stories in English. Joyce said he wrote them in “a style of scrupulous meanness”. The last pages, with snow falling all over Ireland, deserve to be read slowly.'],
  [4217,'A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man','James Joyce','Modern','shelves','Stephen Dedalus grows up in Catholic Ireland, and the language of the book grows up with him, from nursery talk to the arguments of a proud young aesthete. It ends with Stephen leaving to forge “the uncreated conscience of my race”. The sermon on hell in the middle is famously terrifying.'],
  [541,'The Age of Innocence','Edith Wharton','Society','shelves','In the New York of the 1870s, Newland Archer is engaged to the perfect May Welland and falls in love with her unconventional cousin, the Countess Olenska. Wharton won the Pulitzer Prize for it in 1921, the first woman to do so. Every dinner party in it is a battle fought with good manners.'],
  [284,'The House of Mirth','Edith Wharton','Society','shelves','Lily Bart is beautiful, twenty-nine, unmarried and running out of money, in a society that values only the first and last of those. Wharton follows her with great sympathy and no mercy at all. It is as sharp about money as anything by Balzac.'],
  [2610,'Notre-Dame de Paris','Victor Hugo','History','shelves','In medieval Paris, the bell-ringer Quasimodo, the dancer Esmeralda and the archdeacon Frollo circle around the great cathedral, which is the real hero of the book. Hugo wrote it partly to stop Gothic buildings being pulled down, and it helped. English readers know it as The Hunchback of Notre-Dame.'],
  [2542,'A Doll’s House','Henrik Ibsen','Drama','shelves','Nora Helmer seems the perfect cheerful wife, until a signature she forged years ago comes back to call. The final scene, and the door that closes at the end of it, caused a sensation across Europe in 1879. It is short enough for one evening and is still argued over.'],
  [600,'Notes from the Underground','Fyodor Dostoyevsky','Conscience','shelves','A retired official in St Petersburg speaks to us from his “underground”, raging against reason, progress and, above all, himself. Short, bitter and in places very funny, it is often called the first existentialist novel. You will not like the narrator; you may recognise him.'],
  [7849,'The Trial','Franz Kafka','Strange','shelves','Josef K. is arrested one morning, on his thirtieth birthday, without having done anything wrong, and is never told the charge. Kafka left the novel unfinished and asked for it to be burned; his friend Max Brod published it instead. The parable “Before the Law”, told inside it, is worth reading twice.'],
  [3825,'Pygmalion','George Bernard Shaw','Drama','shelves','A professor of phonetics bets that he can pass off a Covent Garden flower seller as a duchess by teaching her to speak “properly”. Shaw’s comedy is far sharper about class than the musical it later became. Eliza, in the end, is the one who has learned something.'],
  [2413,'Madame Bovary','Gustave Flaubert','Society','shelves','Emma Bovary, married to a dull country doctor, has read too many romantic novels and tries to live like their heroines, with lovers and debts. Flaubert was put on trial for its immorality in 1857, and acquitted. He spent years on its sentences, and it shows.'],
  [1079,'The Life and Opinions of Tristram Shandy, Gentleman','Laurence Sterne','Comedy','shelves','The narrator sets out to tell the story of his life and does not manage to be born until the third volume. Sterne’s novel includes a black page, a marbled page and a great many digressions about noses. It is proof that experimental fiction is as old as the novel itself.'],
  [6593,'History of Tom Jones, a Foundling','Henry Fielding','Comedy','shelves','A baby is found in a squire’s bed, grows up handsome, good-hearted and forever in trouble, and is turned out onto the roads of eighteenth-century England. Fielding talks to his reader at the start of every book, like a host at a long dinner. It is one of the great comic novels in the language.'],
  [2130,'Utopia','Thomas More','Philosophy','shelves','A traveller describes an island where property is shared, gold is used for chamber pots, and everyone works six hours a day. More wrote it in Latin in 1516, and gave us the word, which means “no place”. Whether he meant any of it seriously has been argued about ever since.'],
  [1041,'Shakespeare’s Sonnets','William Shakespeare','Poetry','shelves','A hundred and fifty-four sonnets on love, time, beauty and betrayal, addressed to a fair young man and a mysterious dark lady. Nobody knows who either of them was, which has never stopped anyone guessing. Start with 18, 29 and 116, and then read the rest.'],
  [5000,'The Notebooks of Leonardo da Vinci','Leonardo da Vinci','Wonder','shelves','Leonardo’s notes on painting, anatomy, flight, water, light and a thousand other things, translated from his mirror-writing. They read like the thoughts of a man who could not stop asking how everything worked. Keep it by you and open it anywhere.'],
  [58585,'The Prophet','Kahlil Gibran','Philosophy','shelves','A prophet about to sail home is asked by the people of the city to speak of love, marriage, children, work, joy and sorrow, and he answers each in turn. Published in 1923, it has never been out of print. It is short, and best read one chapter at a time.'],
  [786,'Hard Times','Charles Dickens','Society','shelves','In the smoky mill town of Coketown, Mr Gradgrind raises his children on facts and nothing but facts, and learns too late what that has cost them. It is the shortest of Dickens’s novels and his angriest about industrial England. The circus people are the only ones with any sense.'],
  [2638,'The Idiot','Fyodor Dostoyevsky','Conscience','shelves','Prince Myshkin, gentle, honest and just back from a Swiss sanatorium, arrives in St Petersburg society and is taken for a fool. Dostoyevsky wanted to portray a truly good man and see what the world would do to him. The answer is not comforting, but the book is unforgettable.'],
  [64317,'The Great Gatsby','F. Scott Fitzgerald','Modern','shelves','Nick Carraway rents a house on Long Island next to the mansion of Jay Gatsby, who throws enormous parties for the sake of one woman across the bay. It came into the public domain in the United States in 2021. The green light at the end of the dock is still shining.'],
  [63107,'Mrs. Dalloway','Virginia Woolf','Modern','shelves','One June day in London in 1923: Clarissa Dalloway buys the flowers herself and prepares for her party, while across the city a shell-shocked soldier, Septimus Smith, struggles to hold on. Woolf moves between their minds as Big Ben strikes the hours. The two never meet, and they are the book’s two halves.'],
  [2383,'The Canterbury Tales','Geoffrey Chaucer','Legend','shelves','A company of pilgrims riding to Canterbury agrees to tell stories on the way, with a free supper as the prize. Knights, millers, nuns and the much-married Wife of Bath all speak in their own voices, some of them rudely. Read it aloud if you can; it was made to be heard.'],
  [228,'The Aeneid','Virgil','Epic','shelves','Aeneas escapes burning Troy with his father on his back and his son by the hand, and sails to find a new home in Italy. Virgil wrote it as the founding story of Rome and died before he had finished revising it. The love of Dido, queen of Carthage, in the fourth book, is the part everyone remembers.'],

  [2149,'The Works of Edgar Allan Poe — Volume 3','Edgar Allan Poe','Gothic','shelves','The third volume of the Raven Edition holds the whole of Poe’s only novel, The Narrative of Arthur Gordon Pym, followed by Ligeia, Morella and a few comic tales. Pym begins as a sea story and ends somewhere near the South Pole, in a mist that has puzzled readers ever since. Poe died in Baltimore at forty, in circumstances nobody has ever quite explained.'],
  [2150,'The Works of Edgar Allan Poe — Volume 4','Edgar Allan Poe','Gothic','shelves','The fourth volume of the Raven Edition: more of Poe’s tales, some grotesque, some comic, some simply strange. He liked a hoax as much as a horror, and was not always sure which he was writing. It is the volume to open when you have already read the famous ones.'],
  [2151,'The Works of Edgar Allan Poe — Volume 5','Edgar Allan Poe','Poetry','shelves','The last volume of the Raven Edition holds the poems, among them the one the edition is named after, alongside Hop-Frog and the essay The Poetic Principle. The Raven appeared in 1845 and made Poe famous almost overnight, though hardly any richer. Read it aloud; he meant it to be heard.'],
  // ---------- the Kipling bookcase, in the Grand Hall's south-west corner (kipling-shelf.js): his books in the order he published them ----------
  [1858,'Plain Tales from the Hills','Rudyard Kipling','Stories','kipling','Forty short stories of British India, most of them written for the Civil and Military Gazette in Lahore, where Kipling worked as a young journalist. They came out as a book in 1888, when he was twenty-two, and within two years he was famous in London. Simla gossip, broken engagements and men out of their depth are told in a voice that seems to know far more than it lets on.'],
  [null,'Soldiers Three','Rudyard Kipling','Stories','kipling','Mulvaney the Irishman, Ortheris the Cockney and Learoyd the Yorkshireman are three privates in India who drink, fight, steal a dog and tell tall stories, mostly Mulvaney’s. Kipling first published these tales in 1888, in a paper-covered series sold at Indian railway bookstalls. The three turn up again and again in his early work, and readers took to them as to old friends.'],
  [2876,'The Light That Failed','Rudyard Kipling','Novel','kipling','Dick Heldar draws the war in the Sudan for the illustrated papers, comes home famous, and finds that an old sword cut is slowly taking his sight. He loves Maisie, a painter he grew up with, who will not give up her own work to love him back. It was Kipling’s first novel, in 1891, and he wrote two endings: a happy one for the magazine, and the one he preferred for the book.'],
  [null,'Life’s Handicap','Rudyard Kipling','Stories','kipling','A second gathering of Indian stories, from 1891, darker than the Plain Tales. Here are “The Mark of the Beast”, a horror story about a desecrated temple, and “Without Benefit of Clergy”, the tender, doomed story of an Englishman, Ameera and their son. “The Man Who Was”, about an officer who comes back to his regiment after years as a prisoner in Siberia, is here too.'],
  [null,'Barrack-Room Ballads','Rudyard Kipling','Poetry','kipling','Poems in the voice of the British private soldier, published as a book in 1892: “Danny Deever”, “Tommy”, “Gunga Din”, “Mandalay” and the rest. Kipling wrote them in the soldiers’ own Cockney, dropped aitches and all, and they were a sensation when they first appeared in magazines in 1890. Readers who think they know his views on empire are often surprised by how much of the anger here is on the soldiers’ side.'],
  [1937,'The Second Jungle Book','Rudyard Kipling','Wonder','kipling','Kipling went back to the jungle in 1895 and finished Mowgli’s story: the White Cobra guarding a dead king’s treasure, the red dogs of the Deccan led into the wild bees’ rocks, and the spring when Mowgli grows up and leaves the wolves. Between them are tales from further afield, among them “The Miracle of Purun Bhagat”, about a prime minister who gives everything up to live as a holy man in the Himalaya. The second book is the sadder of the two, and some readers think the better.'],
  [null,'The Day’s Work','Rudyard Kipling','Stories','kipling','Stories from 1898 about people, and machines, doing their jobs: an engineer whose great bridge over the Ganges must survive a flood, a new ship whose plates and rivets learn to work together, an American locomotive finding its place in the roundhouse. Kipling admired competence wherever he found it, and here he gives it voices. “William the Conqueror”, a love story set among the famine relief camps of southern India, is among his best.'],
  [3006,'Stalky & Co.','Rudyard Kipling','Comedy','kipling','Three boys at a school for the sons of army officers on the Devon coast wage a long, inventive war on masters, prefects and a rival study, and nearly always win. Kipling drew it from his own years at the United Services College at Westward Ho!, and Beetle, the bookish one in spectacles, is himself. Stalky was his friend Lionel Dunsterville, who grew up to be a general.'],
  [null,'Traffics and Discoveries','Rudyard Kipling','Stories','kipling','Stories from 1904, when Kipling had left India behind for England and was writing more strangely. “They” is a ghost story of a Sussex house full of children who are never quite seen, written a few years after the death of his own daughter Josephine. “Mrs Bathurst”, about a woman glimpsed in a newsreel at Cape Town, is famous for leaving out nearly everything a reader wants to know.'],
  [557,'Puck of Pook’s Hill','Rudyard Kipling','Wonder','kipling','Two children, Dan and Una, act A Midsummer Night’s Dream three times over in a fairy ring on Midsummer Eve, and call up Puck himself, the last of the People of the Hills left in England. He brings them visitors from their own Sussex valley’s past: Weland the smith of the old gods, a Norman knight, a Roman centurion who served on Hadrian’s Wall. Kipling wrote it in 1906 at Bateman’s, his house at Burwash, and set it in the fields around it.'],
  [556,'Rewards and Fairies','Rudyard Kipling','Wonder','kipling','Puck returns in 1910 with more of Dan and Una’s visitors from the English past, among them Queen Elizabeth, a flint-worker of the Stone Age and the herbalist Nicholas Culpeper. Each story has a poem before and after it, and one of them is “If—”, printed here and soon one of the most quoted poems in English. Kipling said he wrote it with his friend Leander Starr Jameson in mind.'],

  // ---------- the Evening Room ----------
  [7256,'The Gift of the Magi','O. Henry','Society','evening-quick','A young couple in New York with one dollar and eighty-seven cents between them each secretly sells their most precious possession to buy the other a Christmas present. O. Henry is said to have written it in a few hours at a table in a New York tavern. The ending is famous, and it still lands.'],
  [12122,'The Monkey’s Paw','W. W. Jacobs','Ghosts','evening-quick','An old soldier back from India brings a dried monkey’s paw to the Whites’ fireside and warns them that it grants three wishes, at a price. They wish anyway. Jacobs wrote mostly comic stories about sailors; this one has frightened readers for over a century.'],
  [375,'An Occurrence at Owl Creek Bridge','Ambrose Bierce','History','evening-quick','A man stands on a railway bridge in Alabama with a noose around his neck, and the rope breaks. Bierce, who fought in the Civil War himself, tells the rest in a handful of pages. Do not skip to the end.'],
  [11231,'Bartleby, the Scrivener','Herman Melville','Strange','evening-hour','A Wall Street lawyer hires a pale, neat, quiet copyist who, asked one day to check a document, replies: “I would prefer not to.” After that he prefers not to do anything else. Melville’s story is funny, then strange, then very sad.'],
  [60976,'Rip Van Winkle','Washington Irving','Legend','evening-hour','A good-natured idler wanders into the Catskill Mountains, drinks with some strange little men playing ninepins, and sleeps for twenty years, straight through the American Revolution. Irving took an old German folk tale and made it American. Rip’s bewilderment on waking is the best part.'],
  [8147,'The Man Who Would Be King','Rudyard Kipling','Adventure','evening-hour','Two former soldiers and chancers, Dravot and Carnehan, set out from India to make themselves kings of Kafiristan, and very nearly succeed. Kipling frames it as a tale told to a newspaperman on a hot night. It is an adventure, a joke and a judgement on empire all at once.'],
  [902,'The Happy Prince, and Other Tales','Oscar Wilde','Wonder','evening-evening','Five fairy tales Wilde wrote for his sons, among them the golden statue who gives his jewels to the poor with the help of a swallow, and “The Selfish Giant”. They are written for children and read best by grown-ups. Keep a handkerchief nearby for the swallow.'],
  [1093,'The Beast in the Jungle','Henry James','Strange','evening-evening','John Marcher is sure that something rare and terrible is waiting to happen to him, and a woman agrees to wait for it beside him, for years. James’s long story is about a life spent waiting for life to begin. Its last scene, in a cemetery, is one of the most painful he ever wrote.'],
  [1213,'The Man That Corrupted Hadleyburg','Mark Twain','Satire','evening-evening','The town of Hadleyburg is proud of being incorruptible, until a stranger it once insulted leaves behind a sack of gold and a riddle. Twain’s story is a perfect trap, and every respectable citizen walks into it. It is one of his darkest pieces, and one of his funniest.'],
  [844,'The Importance of Being Earnest','Oscar Wilde','Comedy','evening-evening','Two young men invent imaginary people to escape their duties, and both fall for women determined to marry someone called Ernest. Wilde called it “a trivial comedy for serious people”. It may be the most quotable play in English, and it reads in about an hour and a half.'],
  [null,'Benito Cereno','Herman Melville','Sea','evening-evening','An American sealing captain boards a battered Spanish slave ship off the coast of Chile and is welcomed by its nervous, sickly captain and his devoted servant. Nothing aboard is quite what it seems. Melville’s short novel is a masterpiece of suspense, and a searching look at everything its narrator cannot see.'],

  // ---------- the English Reading Room ----------
  [11339,'Aesop’s Fables','Aesop','Legend','learners-1','Hundreds of tiny stories, most only a paragraph long: the tortoise and the hare, the boy who cried wolf, the fox and the grapes. Each one ends with its lesson, so you always know what the story meant. Perfect for reading one or two a day.'],
  [500,'The Adventures of Pinocchio','Carlo Collodi','Wonder','learners-1','A wooden puppet carved by old Geppetto comes to life, and his nose grows every time he tells a lie. He runs away, is tricked, is swallowed by an enormous fish and much more, before he learns to be good. The chapters are short and full of action.'],
  [null,'The Tale of Benjamin Bunny','Beatrix Potter','Wonder','learners-1','Peter Rabbit’s cousin Benjamin takes him back into Mr. McGregor’s garden to fetch the clothes Peter lost there. It goes about as well as you would expect. A very short story in clear, simple English, with a good fright in the middle.'],
  [null,'The Adventures of Buster Bear','Thornton W. Burgess','Wonder','learners-1','Buster Bear comes to live in the Green Forest and learns to catch fish, while Little Joe Otter wonders who is stealing his. Burgess wrote hundreds of these gentle animal stories for children. The sentences are short and friendly, and you learn a little about real animals too.'],
  [271,'Black Beauty','Anna Sewell','Society','learners-2','A horse tells his own life story, from a happy meadow in the country to the hard streets of London, pulling cabs. Anna Sewell wrote it so that people would treat horses more kindly, and it helped to change how they were treated. The language is plain and clear, and the feelings are strong.'],
  [1450,'Pollyanna','Eleanor H. Porter','Society','learners-2','Orphaned Pollyanna goes to live with her strict aunt and plays “the glad game”: finding something to be glad about in everything. Slowly the whole town changes around her. It is warm and simple, and it gave English a word for a cheerful optimist.'],
  [770,'The Story of the Treasure Seekers','E. Nesbit','Wonder','learners-2','The six Bastable children decide to restore their family’s fortune by digging for treasure, becoming detectives, and selling their poetry to a newspaper. One of them tells the story and tries very hard to hide which one. It is very funny, and the English is lively without being hard.'],
  [157,'Daddy-Long-Legs','Jean Webster','Society','learners-3','An orphan girl is sent to college by a rich man she has never met, on one condition: she must write to him every month. The whole book is her letters, full of jokes, little drawings and the new words she is learning. Letters are short, so it is easy to read a little at a time.'],
  [47,'Anne of Avonlea','L. M. Montgomery','Society','learners-3','Anne Shirley, now sixteen, becomes the teacher at the village school and finds that her pupils have as much imagination as she does. If you enjoyed Anne of Green Gables, this is the next step. The descriptions of Prince Edward Island are full of useful words for the natural world.'],
  [498,'Rebecca of Sunnybrook Farm','Kate Douglas Wiggin','Society','learners-3','Talkative, imaginative Rebecca is sent from her family’s poor farm to live with two stern aunts in a small town in Maine. She wins over almost everyone, eventually even the aunts. Her way of speaking is full of expressions worth collecting.'],
  [4276,'North and South','Elizabeth Gaskell','Society','learners-4','Margaret Hale moves from the green south of England to the smoky northern mill town of Milton, and clashes with the proud mill owner John Thornton. Gaskell writes about strikes, class and love all at once. It asks for patience and gives a great deal back.'],
  [6688,'The Mill on the Floss','George Eliot','Society','learners-4','Clever, passionate Maggie Tulliver grows up beside the river Floss with her brother Tom, whom she loves more than anyone. Eliot remembers childhood as well as any writer ever has. The sentences are long but worth it, and the ending is unforgettable.'],
  [7256,'The Gift of the Magi','O. Henry','Society','learners-short',''],
  [null,'The Tale of Tom Kitten','Beatrix Potter','Wonder','learners-short','Mrs. Tabitha Twitchit dresses her three kittens in their best clothes for a tea party and tells them to keep clean. They do not. A five-minute story with easy sentences and some wonderful words, like “pinafore” and “tippet”.'],
  [null,'The Tale of Jemima Puddle-Duck','Beatrix Potter','Wonder','learners-short','A duck who wants to hatch her own eggs goes looking for a quiet nest and meets a very polite gentleman with black ears and a bushy tail. Every reader can see what he is, except Jemima. Short, simple and funny.'],

  // ---------- the night railway: the Signal House reading room ----------
  [null,'The Man in Lower Ten','Mary Roberts Rinehart','Mystery','signal','A young lawyer carrying important papers wakes in the wrong berth of a night sleeper and finds the man in his own berth, lower ten, murdered. Then the train is wrecked. Rinehart’s mystery is fast and funny, and a sleeping car is the ideal place to read it.'],
  [null,'Stories of the Railroad','John A. Hill','Adventure','signal','Tales of American railroad men in the age of steam: runaway engines, snowbound trains and the rivalries of the roundhouse. John A. Hill drove locomotives himself before he became an editor, and later helped found the McGraw-Hill publishing house. He knew exactly what it felt like at the throttle.'],
  [null,'The Wrong Box','Robert Louis Stevenson','Comedy','signal','Two elderly brothers are the last survivors of a tontine, a lottery that pays out to whoever lives longest, and a railway accident sets off a comedy of mislaid bodies. Stevenson wrote it with his stepson, Lloyd Osbourne. It is odd and very funny, and not at all what you expect from the author of Treasure Island.'],
  [null,'The Iron Horse','R. M. Ballantyne','Adventure','signal','Ballantyne’s novel of 1871 about life on the early railway: engine drivers, stokers, signalmen and a runaway train. He liked to learn his subjects first-hand, and his affection for steam is on every page. A signal house seemed the only proper place for it.'],
  [2183,'Three Men on the Bummel','Jerome K. Jerome','Comedy','signal','The three men from the boat, a little older now, take a cycling holiday through Germany, with trains, luggage and a great deal of confusion over the language. Jerome’s observations on the German love of rules are affectionate and very funny. Three Men in a Boat is also in this library, if you would like to start there.'],
  [1298,'The Virginian','Owen Wister','Adventure','signal','A cowboy known only as the Virginian, a schoolteacher from Vermont, and the railway bringing the East to Wyoming. Wister’s novel of 1902 more or less invented the western, including the line “When you call me that, smile!” It begins with the narrator stepping off a train at Medicine Bow.'],
  [null,'The Mystery of the Blue Train','Agatha Christie','Mystery','signal','An American heiress is murdered aboard the luxurious Blue Train from Calais to the Riviera, and her famous rubies are gone. Hercule Poirot happens to be travelling on the same train. Christie wrote it in a hard year and never much liked it, but the train is glorious.'],
  [3177,'Roughing It','Mark Twain','Journey','signal','Young Sam Clemens travels west by stagecoach to Nevada, where he tries silver mining, newspaper work and getting rich, and fails at nearly all of them. It is exaggerated in the best tradition of the tall tale. Before the railway, this is what a long journey felt like.'],

  // ---------- the night railway: the Tidebound Quay reading room ----------
  [2186,'Captains Courageous','Rudyard Kipling','Sea','tide','A spoiled American boy falls off an ocean liner and is picked up by a fishing schooner on the Grand Banks, whose captain will not turn back to port for him. Harvey has to work, and he grows up. Kipling went to Gloucester to learn how the fishermen really lived.'],
  [5658,'Lord Jim','Joseph Conrad','Sea','tide','A young officer abandons a pilgrim ship he believes is sinking, and spends the rest of his life trying to recover from that one moment. Conrad’s Marlow tells the story in pieces, on verandas after dinner. It is a book about honour, and whether a single failure can ever be undone.'],
  [1142,'Typhoon','Joseph Conrad','Sea','tide','Captain MacWhirr, a man with no imagination at all, steers his steamer straight into a typhoon in the China Seas with two hundred passengers below. Conrad, who spent twenty years at sea, wrote the storm from experience. The captain’s stubbornness turns out to be its own kind of courage.'],
  [10743,'Moonfleet','J. Meade Falkner','Adventure','tide','In a Dorset village, young John Trenchard finds a smugglers’ vault beneath the church, and a locket that leads to the lost diamond of Blackbeard. Storms, escapes and a long friendship with an old smuggler follow. It is one of the best adventure stories of the English coast.'],
  [646,'The Coral Island','R. M. Ballantyne','Adventure','tide','Three boys are shipwrecked on a Pacific island and at first live very happily on coconuts and adventure. Ballantyne’s book of 1857 was hugely popular; William Golding wrote Lord of the Flies as a reply to it. Its picture of the Pacific islanders belongs to its time and is not a fair one.'],
  [144,'The Voyage Out','Virginia Woolf','Journey','tide','Rachel Vinrace, twenty-four and sheltered, sails from London to South America on her father’s ship and begins to discover the world and herself. It was Woolf’s first novel. A quay is the right place for a book about leaving.'],
  [null,'Mr. Midshipman Easy','Frederick Marryat','Sea','tide','Jack Easy, raised by a philosopher father to believe that all men are equal, joins the Royal Navy and tries to argue with his captain. Marryat was a naval captain himself and wrote with gusto about life aboard a warship in the Napoleonic Wars. It is lively, silly and full of the sea.'],
  [null,'The Wreck of the Grosvenor','W. Clark Russell','Sea','tide','A young second mate finds the crew of his merchant ship close to mutiny because the food is rotten, and the voyage goes from bad to worse. Clark Russell had been a sailor himself, and this became one of the great Victorian bestsellers of the sea. The storms are very real.'],

  // ---------- Mars: the Reading Room of Helium ----------
  [64,'The Gods of Mars','Edgar Rice Burroughs','Adventure','mars','John Carter returns to Barsoom and lands in the Valley Dor, the Martian heaven, only to find it is a trap kept by a false religion. Burroughs’s second Mars book is his angriest and one of his most inventive: white apes, plant men and the black pirates of the south. It ends on a cliffhanger that kept its first readers waiting for months.'],
  [68,'The Warlord of Mars','Edgar Rice Burroughs','Adventure','mars','John Carter chases the villains who have carried off Dejah Thoris across the whole of Barsoom, from the southern pole to the frozen north. It completes the first Barsoom trilogy and is pure momentum. The hothouse cities of the yellow men, under their glass domes in the ice, are among Burroughs’s best inventions.'],
  [null,'The Chessmen of Mars','Edgar Rice Burroughs','Adventure','mars','Princess Tara of Helium is blown off course in a storm and lands among the Kaldanes, living heads that ride about on headless bodies. Then there is jetan, Martian chess, played with living pieces and to the death. Burroughs printed the rules at the back of the book, so you can play it at home, more gently.'],
  [19141,'Edison’s Conquest of Mars','Garrett P. Serviss','Speculative','mars','Wells’s Martians have been beaten, and the nations of Earth send a fleet of electric ships, designed by Thomas Edison, to strike back. Garrett P. Serviss wrote it as a newspaper serial in 1898, within months of The War of the Worlds and without asking Wells. It has some of the earliest space suits and space battles in fiction.'],
  [10165,'Across the Zodiac','Percy Greg','Speculative','mars','A traveller builds a ship driven by “apergy”, a force that pushes against gravity, and flies to Mars, where he finds an orderly, rather joyless society and a great deal of bureaucracy. Percy Greg’s novel of 1880 is one of the first stories of a voyage to Mars. His ship was called the Astronaut, perhaps the first use of the word.'],
  [47015,'Mars','Percival Lowell','Wonder','mars','The Boston astronomer Percival Lowell built an observatory in Arizona to watch Mars, and saw a network of fine straight lines he believed were canals, dug by a people saving their last water. This is his case, argued carefully and with his own drawings. The canals were not there, but this book fed nearly every Mars story on this shelf.'],
  [10855,'Is Mars Habitable?','Alfred Russel Wallace','Wonder','mars','In 1907 Alfred Russel Wallace, who had worked out natural selection alongside Darwin, read Percival Lowell’s Mars and Its Canals and answered it with this short book. He calculated that Mars was far too cold and its air far too thin for water to flow, let alone for engineers to dig canals across it. Shelve it beside Lowell and let them argue.'],
  [604,'Gulliver of Mars','Edwin Lester Arnold','Adventure','mars','A lieutenant of the United States Navy unrolls a strange carpet and finds himself on Mars, among a pleasant, idle people and the barbarians who threaten them. Edwin Lester Arnold’s novel sank without trace in 1905 as Lieut. Gullivar Jones: His Vacation, and came back in 1964 as Gulliver of Mars. It is read now as a forerunner of Burroughs’s Barsoom, which appeared seven years after it.'],
  [null,'The Certainty of a Future Life in Mars','L. P. Gratacap','Speculative','mars','A father promises his son that after death he will send word from Mars, where he believes souls go next, and the messages arrive by wireless. Louis Pope Gratacap was a curator at the American Museum of Natural History. It is strange and earnest, and it is the reason the wireless set in the ruin is kept switched on.'],

  // ---------- the Periodicals Room: magazines and papers as they first appeared ----------
  [null,'Punch, or the London Charivari, Volume 1, July 17, 1841','Various','Comedy','periodicals','The first volume of Punch, the London comic weekly founded in 1841 by Henry Mayhew and Ebenezer Landells. It gave the word “cartoon” its modern meaning, printed Thackeray, and kept going for a century and a half. Early Punch is rowdier and more radical than its later reputation.'],
  [41613,'The Strand Magazine, Vol. 01, January 1891','Various','Mystery','periodicals','The first number of The Strand Magazine. Within six months it was printing short stories about a new detective called Sherlock Holmes, and its sales rose with every one. Its founder, George Newnes, wanted a picture on every page, and very nearly managed it.'],
  [null,'The Atlantic Monthly, Vol. 01, No. 01, November, 1857','Various','Society','periodicals','The first number of The Atlantic Monthly, founded in Boston in 1857 by a circle that included Emerson, Longfellow, Oliver Wendell Holmes and Harriet Beecher Stowe. Its pieces were unsigned, so its first readers had to guess who had written what. The magazine is still published.'],
  [null,'Notes and Queries, Number 01, November 3, 1849','Various','Wonder','periodicals','The first number of Notes and Queries, a weekly founded in 1849 so that readers could put obscure questions to one another and answer them. Its motto came from Captain Cuttle in Dickens’s Dombey and Son: “When found, make a note of.” It is still running, as a scholarly journal.'],
  [41875,'The Yellow Book, an Illustrated Quarterly. Vol. 1, April 1894','Henry Harland','Modern','periodicals','The first volume of The Yellow Book, the notorious quarterly of 1894, with drawings by Aubrey Beardsley and a story by Henry James. It was yellow because French novels were, and French novels were wicked. When Oscar Wilde was arrested he was said to be carrying a yellow book, and the magazine never recovered, although he never wrote for it.'],
  [null,'The Germ: Thoughts towards Nature in Poetry, Literature and Art','Various','Poetry','periodicals','The magazine of the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood, which lasted four numbers in 1850 and sold almost nothing. It printed Dante Gabriel Rossetti’s The Blessed Damozel and early poems by Christina Rossetti. Few magazines have failed so beautifully.'],
  [null,'The Idler Magazine, Vol. III, May 1893','Various','Comedy','periodicals','The Idler, a monthly founded in 1892 by Jerome K. Jerome and Robert Barr for light, clever reading. Conan Doyle, Kipling, Mark Twain and Bret Harte all wrote for it. The editors’ gossipy column, The Idlers’ Club, was half the reason people bought it.'],
  [null,'St. Nicholas Magazine for Boys and Girls, Vol. 5, No. 5, March, 1878','Various','Wonder','periodicals','St. Nicholas, the great American children’s magazine, edited for over thirty years by Mary Mapes Dodge, the author of Hans Brinker. Louisa May Alcott, Mark Twain and Rudyard Kipling all wrote for it. Generations of American children grew up waiting for it each month.'],
  [null,'Harper\'s Young People, January 20, 1880','Various','Wonder','periodicals','Harper’s Young People, a weekly for children launched in 1879, full of serials, puzzles, games and letters from readers across America. It is a window on what a Victorian childhood was supposed to enjoy, and sometimes did.'],
  [8951,'Scientific American, Volume 17, No. 26, December 28, 1867','Various','Speculative','periodicals','Scientific American in its weekly years, when it was chiefly a journal of inventions: new engines, safety devices and improved machinery, each with an engraving. Its publishers also ran a patent agency, so the inventors came to them. Browse it for the future as 1867 imagined it.'],
  [null,'Blackwood\'s Edinburgh Magazine, Vol. 55, No. 339, January, 1844','Various','Society','periodicals','Blackwood’s Edinburgh Magazine, “Maga” to its readers: Tory, combative and literary, with fiction, travel and politics sharing the same double columns. George Eliot’s first fiction appeared in its pages in 1857. Its readers argued about it almost as much as they read it.'],
  [13865,'Chambers\'s Edinburgh Journal, No. 418','Various','Society','periodicals','Chambers’s Edinburgh Journal, a cheap, respectable weekly of useful knowledge founded by the brothers William and Robert Chambers in 1832. It aimed to give working readers good reading for a penny and a half. It is a fine picture of what ordinary Victorians learned from.'],
  [null,'Godey\'s Lady\'s Book, Vol. 42, January, 1851','Various','Society','periodicals','Godey’s Lady’s Book, the most popular American magazine of the mid-nineteenth century, with hand-coloured fashion plates, recipes, patterns and fiction. Its editor, Sarah Josepha Hale, campaigned for years to make Thanksgiving a national holiday, and won. She also wrote “Mary Had a Little Lamb”.'],
  [null,'The Crisis, Vol. 1, No. 1, November, 1910','Various','Contested','periodicals','The first number of The Crisis, the magazine of the NAACP, founded and edited by W. E. B. Du Bois in 1910. It reported lynching, discrimination and achievement with equal force, and later published the young writers of the Harlem Renaissance. A thousand copies of this first number were printed.'],
  [41481,'Astounding Stories of Super-Science, January 1930','Harry Bates','Speculative','periodicals','The very first issue of Astounding Stories of Super-Science: pulp paper, a lurid cover and adventure among the planets. Within a decade, as Astounding Science Fiction, it would be the magazine that made modern science fiction. Here it is before it grew up.'],
  [36745,'McClure\'s Magazine, Vol. 1, No. 1','Various','Society','periodicals','The first number of McClure’s Magazine, sold for fifteen cents when its rivals cost twenty-five or thirty-five. Within ten years it had helped invent investigative journalism, with Ida Tarbell’s history of Standard Oil. This first number is gentler: interviews, stories and a great deal of hope.'],
  [null,'The Mirror of Literature, Amusement, and Instruction, Vol. 10, No. 287, December 22, 1827','Various','Wonder','periodicals','The Mirror of Literature, Amusement, and Instruction, a twopenny London weekly of the 1820s: engravings, curiosities, extracts from new books and odd facts. It was one of the first cheap illustrated magazines, and it reads like a cabinet of curiosities made of paper.'],
  [9334,'The Spectator, Volume 1','Richard Steele and Joseph Addison','Philosophy','periodicals','The Spectator of 1711 and 1712, a daily sheet written chiefly by Joseph Addison and Richard Steele, in which the silent Mr. Spectator watches London from its coffee houses. It set out to bring philosophy “out of closets and libraries” to tea tables and coffee houses. It taught the eighteenth century how to write an essay.'],
  [null,'The Continental Monthly, Vol. 1, No. 1, January, 1862','Various','History','periodicals','The Continental Monthly, founded in 1862, in the middle of the American Civil War, to argue for the Union and against slavery. Its essays and stories were written for readers who knew the war was going on outside their windows.'],
  [null,'Birds, Illustrated by Color Photography, Vol. 1, No. 1, January 1897','Various','Wonder','periodicals','Birds, Illustrated by Color Photography, a Chicago monthly of 1897 and one of the first magazines to print photographs in colour. The birds were mounted specimens posed on branches, which is why some of them look a little startled. Several pieces are written as if the bird itself were speaking.'],
  [null,'The Great Round World and What Is Going On In It, Vol. 1, No. 1, November 4, 1896','Various','History','periodicals','A weekly newspaper for young people, begun in 1896: the week’s news explained plainly, from the war in Cuba to the troubles in Crete. It is the nearest thing in this room to a real newspaper, and a good way to find out what the world talked about that autumn.'],
  [24477,'The Nursery, April 1873, Vol. XIII.','Various','Wonder','periodicals','The Nursery, a Boston monthly for the youngest readers, with large type, short stories and a picture on nearly every page. Simple, gentle and easy to read, it is a good place to start for anyone learning English, too.'],

  // ---------- the Map Room (map-room.js): voyages of discovery ----------
  [74723,'The First Voyage Round the World','Antonio Pigafetta','Journey','map-voyages','Antonio Pigafetta sailed with Magellan in 1519 and was one of eighteen men who came home on the Victoria three years later. His journal records the strait, the crossing of the Pacific and Magellan’s death in the Philippines. He also noted words from the languages he heard on the way.'],
  [944,'The Voyage of the Beagle','Charles Darwin','Natural History','map-voyages','Darwin’s voyage joined coastlines, specimens and observations into a book that changed how people understood the natural world. Follow the ship’s route on the maps, then open the book to see what he noticed at each stop. The journal makes the connection between a place and what a traveller can learn there.'],
  [3752,'Voyager’s Tales','Richard Hakluyt','Journey','map-voyages','Richard Hakluyt spent his life gathering accounts of English voyages, and published them in his great collection of navigations. This short selection lets readers dip into those accounts without taking on the whole collection. Hakluyt was a clergyman who sailed on none of the voyages himself.'],
  [16565,'History of the Expedition under the Command of Captains Lewis and Clark, Vol. 1.','Meriwether Lewis and William Clark','Journey','map-voyages','In 1804 Meriwether Lewis and William Clark led the Corps of Discovery up the Missouri, over the Rocky Mountains to the Pacific and back again. The expedition crossed Indigenous homelands during the westward expansion of the United States. Read the survey alongside the maps that helped make that expansion possible.'],
  [2440,'The Naturalist on the River Amazons','Henry Walter Bates','Science','map-voyages','Henry Walter Bates spent eleven years on the Amazon and sent home more than fourteen thousand species, eight thousand of them new to science. He had set out with Alfred Russel Wallace, and Darwin called this the best book of natural history travels ever published in England. It is just as good on the people he lived among and the river towns.'],
  [11579,'Scott’s Last Expedition','Robert Falcon Scott','Journey','map-voyages','Scott reached the South Pole in January 1912 to find that Amundsen had been there five weeks before. He went on writing in the tent on the way back until the end of March, and his journals were found with his body that November. Few books have an ending so well known or so hard to read.'],
  [20923,'A Negro Explorer at the North Pole','Matthew Henson','Journey','map-voyages','Matthew Henson went north with Robert Peary on seven expeditions over some twenty years, and was with him when they claimed to have reached the North Pole. His own short account was published in 1912 and was long overlooked. Whether they reached the Pole is still disputed, but Henson’s role deserves to be read in his own words.'],
  [11039,'A woman’s journey round the world','Ida Pfeiffer','Journey','map-voyages','Ida Pfeiffer travelled from Vienna through Brazil, Chile, Tahiti, China, India, Persia and Asia Minor, often with little money and no companion. Her account gives this shelf a traveller’s own view of a journey that crosses half the globe. It is an engrossing counterpoint to the expedition accounts written by men.'],
  [26006,'A Girl’s Ride in Iceland','Mrs. Alec-Tweedie','Journey','map-voyages','Ethel Alec-Tweedie and her companions ride across Iceland in the late nineteenth century, facing rough seas, difficult roads and changeable weather. Her account follows a route far from the destinations most tourists then chose. It brings the map’s empty spaces down to the scale of a horse and a road.'],
  [2530,'The Malay Archipelago, Volume 1','Alfred Russel Wallace','Natural History','map-voyages','Wallace’s eight years of travel through the islands of Southeast Asia combine field notes, wildlife and changing coastlines. Read with an awareness that this nineteenth-century European account reflects its author’s perspective. The places and people he describes had lives and histories beyond the expedition.'],
  [66063,'The Open Polar Sea','I. I. Hayes','Journey','map-voyages','Isaac Israel Hayes’s account follows an Arctic voyage aboard the schooner United States through ice and Greenland. The crew hoped to find an open sea in the far north. Its map shows both the expedition’s route and the limits of what its travellers knew.'],

  // These earlier arrivals remain in the general shelves after the Map Room was curated.
  [77820,'Select Letters of Christopher Columbus','Christopher Columbus','Journey','shelves','Columbus’s letters record four voyages and the European imperial perspective from which he described the places he encountered. Those places already had their own peoples and histories. Read his claims beside the maps that later used his accounts.'],
  [36924,'The Letters of Amerigo Vespucci','Amerigo Vespucci','Journey','shelves','The letters of the Florentine merchant whose name ended up on two continents describe voyages to the coast of South America around 1500. Whether he made every voyage he describes has been argued over for five hundred years. Waldseemüller’s 1507 map gave his name to the New World, and it hangs on the north wall.'],
  [2991,'Sir Francis Drake’s Famous Voyage Round the World','Francis Pretty','Journey','shelves','Francis Pretty, one of Drake’s gentlemen at arms, wrote this account of the voyage of 1577 to 1580. It follows the ship through the Strait of Magellan, raids on Spanish treasure ships and a summer on the coast of California. Hakluyt printed Pretty’s account in his Principal Navigations.'],
  [2272,'The Discovery of Guiana','Walter Raleigh','Journey','shelves','In 1595 Sir Walter Raleigh sailed up the Orinoco in search of El Dorado and the golden city of Manoa, and returned with stories instead of gold. His book, published the next year, is a courtier’s sales pitch and a fine piece of prose. For two centuries afterwards, maps showed a lake of Manoa where no lake exists.'],
  [3284,'The Discovery of the Source of the Nile','John Hanning Speke','Journey','shelves','In 1858 John Hanning Speke reached the great lake he named Victoria and decided it was the source of the Nile. This is the journey of 1860 to 1863 that went back to prove it, despite very little evidence at first. Richard Burton, his old companion, disputed the claim in public for years, and Speke was right.'],

  // ---------- the Map Room: maps and their makers ----------
  [14291,'The Story of Geographical Discovery','Joseph Jacobs','History','map-makers','Joseph Jacobs, better known for collecting English fairy tales, wrote this short history of how the world became known in 1899. It runs from the ancient world to the explorers of his own day, and the maps get larger as it goes. It is a good first book for this room, and a quick one.'],
  [58349,'History of Geography','J. Scott Keltie','History','map-makers','A compact history of geography from the Greeks to the beginning of the twentieth century, by John Scott Keltie, secretary of the Royal Geographical Society, written with O. J. R. Howarth. It appeared in 1913 in a series of short histories of the sciences. Ptolemy, Mercator and the great surveys all have their pages.'],
  [44884,'The Geography of Strabo','Strabo','History','map-makers','Strabo, a Greek from Amaseia in Pontus, wrote his seventeen books of geography under Augustus and Tiberius, describing the whole world the Romans knew, region by region. He argues with earlier geographers on nearly every page, which is half the pleasure. This is the first of three volumes in the translation by H. C. Hamilton and W. Falconer.'],
  [68946,'Pausanias’ Description of Greece','Pausanias','Journey','map-makers','Pausanias, a Greek from Asia Minor, travelled round Greece in the second century AD and wrote what amounts to the first guidebook to it, temple by temple and statue by statue. Archaeologists still dig with him in hand, and he has led them to more than one lost site. This is the first volume of A. R. Shilleto’s translation.'],
  [18757,'Prince Henry the Navigator','C. Raymond Beazley','History','map-makers','Henry of Portugal, a son of King John I, sent ship after ship down the coast of West Africa in the fifteenth century, though he seldom went to sea himself. Beazley’s life of him, published in 1895, is also an account of geography through the Middle Ages, from the pilgrims to the chartmakers. The famous school of navigators he is supposed to have kept at Sagres has since been doubted.'],
  [21915,'Directions for Navigating on Part of the South Coast of Newfoundland','James Cook','Sea','map-makers','Before he sailed to the Pacific, James Cook spent five summers surveying the coasts of Newfoundland, and his charts were so good that they were still in use a century later. These are the sailing directions he published to go with one of them. It is short, practical and full of soundings, and it is where the great navigator made his name.'],
  [14999,'A Briefe Introduction to Geography','William Pemble','Science','map-makers','William Pemble, a young Puritan lecturer at Oxford, died in 1623, and this little book of his was published some years later. It is a short course in the geography of his day, the kind a student would have learned beside a globe in the age of Mercator. The spelling is old, and so is the confidence.'],

  // ---------- the Map Room: lands not on any map ----------
  [2434,'New Atlantis','Francis Bacon','Philosophy','map-lands','Sailors lost in the South Sea come upon Bensalem, an island unknown to the world, whose great institution, Salomon’s House, is a college for the study of nature. Bacon left it unfinished, and it was published in 1627, the year after his death. The founders of the Royal Society, thirty-three years later, took it as something like a plan.'],
  [2816,'The City of the Sun','Tommaso Campanella','Philosophy','map-lands','A Genoese sea captain tells a Knight Hospitaller of a city on an island beyond the Indian Ocean, built in seven rings, where everything is held in common and the walls are painted with all the knowledge of the world. Campanella, a Dominican friar, wrote it in 1602 in a prison in Naples, where he spent twenty-seven years. It is a utopia by a man who had every reason to want one.'],
  [null,'Sylvie and Bruno Concluded','Lewis Carroll','Wonder','map-lands','The second half of Carroll’s last novel, published in 1893, in which a visitor called Mein Herr describes his country’s map, made on the scale of a mile to the mile. The farmers objected that it would cover the country and shut out the sunlight, so now they use the country itself as its own map, and it does nearly as well. Borges told a sterner version of the same story half a century later.'],
  [782,'The Travels of Sir John Mandeville','John Mandeville','Wonder','map-lands','In the late Middle Ages this was one of the most popular books in Europe: a knight’s journey to Jerusalem, India and Cathay, past dog-headed men and the fountain of youth. Most of it was borrowed from other books, and the knight himself may never have existed. For two centuries it was read as a true account.'],
  // ---------- the Set Texts Room (set-texts-room.js): the Shakespeare plays set for GCSE ----------
  [1533,'Macbeth','William Shakespeare','Drama','set-texts','A Scottish general meets three witches on a heath who tell him he will be king, and he and his wife decide not to wait. Written around 1606, it is Shakespeare’s shortest tragedy, and it moves at a terrible speed from one murder to the next. Actors still call it the Scottish play rather than say its name in a theatre.'],
  [1540,'The Tempest','William Shakespeare','Drama','set-texts','Prospero, the overthrown Duke of Milan, has lived twelve years on an island with his daughter Miranda, the spirit Ariel and Caliban, and he raises a storm to wreck his enemies on its shore. Written around 1611, it is probably the last play Shakespeare wrote on his own. It ends with Prospero giving up his magic and asking the audience to set him free.'],
  [1515,'The Merchant of Venice','William Shakespeare','Drama','set-texts','Antonio borrows three thousand ducats from the moneylender Shylock to help a friend court Portia, and signs a bond for a pound of his own flesh if he cannot repay. Written in the late 1590s, it is a comedy and one of Shakespeare’s most uncomfortable plays. Portia makes her speech on the quality of mercy in a courtroom, disguised as a young lawyer.'],
  [1519,'Much Ado About Nothing','William Shakespeare','Drama','set-texts','Beatrice and Benedick swear they will never marry anyone, least of all each other, while their friends Hero and Claudio are nearly parted by a lie. Written around 1598, it is Shakespeare’s comedy of quick tongues. The constable Dogberry, who gets every long word wrong, uncovers the plot by accident.'],
  [1522,'Julius Caesar','William Shakespeare','Drama','set-texts','Brutus, Cassius and their fellow senators stab Caesar on the Ides of March to save the Roman republic, and then have to answer to Mark Antony and the crowd. It was seen at the new Globe in 1599, one of the first plays performed there. The library also has it in Latin, in the Latin Reading Room.'],

  // ---------- the Lost Property Office (book-lift.js): unclaimed, unsorted, a few brought out at random each visit ----------
  [3330,'The Analects of Confucius','Confucius','Philosophy','lost-property','The sayings of Confucius and his disciples, gathered by their followers after his death in 479 BC and for two thousand years at the heart of a Chinese education. Most entries are a few lines long: a question, an answer, now and then a rebuke. It is a book to open anywhere.'],
  [10800,'The Anatomy of Melancholy','Robert Burton','Essays','lost-property','Robert Burton, an Oxford scholar who signed himself Democritus Junior, set out to explain melancholy and ended by writing about nearly everything: love, diet, air, scholars, religion and the cures for sorrow. First published in 1621, it kept growing in every edition he lived to see. Samuel Johnson said it was the only book that ever took him out of bed two hours sooner than he wished to rise.'],
  [1156,'Babbitt','Sinclair Lewis','Novel','lost-property','George F. Babbitt sells real estate in the booming Midwestern city of Zenith, believes everything he is told to believe, and is quietly miserable. Sinclair Lewis’s novel of 1922 gave the language a word for a self-satisfied man of business. In 1930 Lewis became the first American to win the Nobel Prize in Literature.'],
  [2686,'The Book of Snobs','William Makepeace Thackeray','Satire','lost-property','Thackeray’s sketches for Punch in 1846 and 1847, sorting the snobs of England into kinds: royal, military, clerical, university, city and country. They first appeared as by “One of Themselves”, and he means it. Vanity Fair, which followed, is the same joke at the length of a novel.'],
  [769,'The Book of Tea','Kakuzō Okakura','Essays','lost-property','Written in English in 1906 by Okakura Kakuzō, a Japanese scholar then working at the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston. It is about tea, and through tea about Taoism, Zen, flowers and the beauty of things left imperfect. It is short enough to finish before the pot goes cold.'],
  [8868,'Botchan','Natsume Sōseki','Novel','lost-property','A hot-headed young man from Tokyo takes a post teaching mathematics at a country school on Shikoku, where his pupils torment him and his colleagues outmanoeuvre him. Natsume Sōseki wrote it in 1906, out of his own year as a teacher in Matsuyama. It is still one of the most widely read novels in Japan.'],
  [8800,'The Divine Comedy','Dante Alighieri','Poetry','lost-property','Midway through his life, in a dark wood, Dante loses his way, and is led down through Hell and up the mountain of Purgatory by Virgil, and through Paradise by Beatrice. He wrote it in exile from Florence, in Italian rather than Latin, early in the fourteenth century. It made the Tuscan he wrote in the model for a whole language.'],
  [37134,'The Elements of Style','William Strunk','Reference','lost-property','William Strunk Jr. had this little guide printed for his English students at Cornell in 1918. “Omit needless words,” it says, and then does. One of those students, E. B. White, revised it forty years later and made it famous; this is the original.'],
  [2447,'Eminent Victorians','Lytton Strachey','Biography','lost-property','Four short lives, of Cardinal Manning, Florence Nightingale, Thomas Arnold of Rugby and General Gordon, written in 1918 with an irreverence the Victorians would not have allowed. Strachey changed biography by choosing what to leave out. Nightingale comes off best, and not as a lady with a lamp.'],
  [4517,'Ethan Frome','Edith Wharton','Novel','lost-property','A poor farmer in a frozen New England village, his ailing, complaining wife, and her young cousin who comes to help in the house. Edith Wharton’s short novel of 1911 moves slowly towards a sledding hill. It is the bleakest book she wrote, and among the best.'],
  [7164,'Gitanjali','Rabindranath Tagore','Poetry','lost-property','“Song offerings”: Tagore’s own English prose versions of his Bengali poems, published in London in 1912 with an introduction by W. B. Yeats. The next year Tagore became the first writer from outside Europe to win the Nobel Prize in Literature. The poems speak to God, or to a beloved, or to both at once.'],
  [19,'The Song of Hiawatha','Henry Wadsworth Longfellow','Poetry','lost-property','Longfellow’s long poem of 1855, set among the Ojibwe on the southern shore of Lake Superior, in a drumming metre he borrowed from the Finnish Kalevala. It drew on Henry Rowe Schoolcraft’s collections of Ojibwe stories, and on Longfellow’s imagination at least as much. It was an enormous success, and enormously parodied.'],
  [2946,'Howards End','E. M. Forster','Novel','lost-property','Two sisters full of ideas, a family of businessmen with none, and a house in Hertfordshire that ought to belong to whoever loves it best. Forster’s novel of 1910 carries the motto “Only connect”. A bank clerk’s umbrella, taken by mistake at a concert, starts most of the trouble.'],
  [2160,'The Expedition of Humphry Clinker','Tobias Smollett','Novel','lost-property','A grumbling Welsh squire tours Britain with his household, and each of them writes letters home describing the same places entirely differently. Humphry Clinker is the ragged postilion they pick up on the road. It was Smollett’s last novel, published in 1771, the year he died.'],
  [5670,'Jacob’s Room','Virginia Woolf','Novel','lost-property','A life told from its edges: Jacob Flanders as a boy at the seaside, at Cambridge, in London and in Greece, always seen by someone else. Virginia Woolf’s third novel, of 1922, was her first clean break with the ordinary novel. The empty room at the end says what the book will not.'],
  [7700,'Lysistrata','Aristophanes','Drama','lost-property','Athens and Sparta have been at war for twenty years, so the women of Greece, led by Lysistrata, seize the treasury and refuse their husbands until they make peace. Aristophanes put it on in 411 BC, in the middle of the real war. It is still very rude and still very funny.'],
  [370,'Moll Flanders','Daniel Defoe','Novel','lost-property','Born in Newgate prison, Moll was, the title page says, twelve years a whore, five times a wife (once to her own brother), twelve years a thief and eight years a transported felon in Virginia. Defoe published her life in 1722 as if she had written it herself. She tells it with a businesslike eye on what everything cost.'],
  [20058,'The Napoleon of Notting Hill','G. K. Chesterton','Novel','lost-property','In the London of 1984 the king is chosen by lot, and the lot falls on Auberon Quin, a joker who decrees that every borough shall have its walls, halberds and heralds. Only Adam Wayne, the young Provost of Notting Hill, takes it seriously, and goes to war over a street. It was Chesterton’s first novel, in 1904.'],
  [351,'Of Human Bondage','W. Somerset Maugham','Novel','lost-property','Philip Carey, an orphan with a club foot, is brought up by his uncle, a vicar, and tries school, Heidelberg, painting in Paris and medicine in London before he learns what he wants. Maugham’s long novel of 1915 is largely his own life. Mildred, the waitress Philip cannot stop loving, is one of fiction’s great bad choices.'],
  [34901,'On Liberty','John Stuart Mill','Philosophy','lost-property','Mill’s essay of 1859 argues that the only reason for which power may rightly be used over anyone, against their will, is to prevent harm to others. He dedicated it to the memory of his wife, Harriet Taylor Mill, whose thinking he said was in all of it. Its chapter on freedom of thought is quoted whenever that freedom is in trouble.'],
  [18269,'Pensées','Blaise Pascal','Philosophy','lost-property','The notes Pascal left at his death in 1662 for a defence of Christianity he never finished, published afterwards as his Thoughts. Some run to a page and some to a line: “The heart has its reasons, which reason does not know.” Here too are the wager and the thinking reed.'],
  [131,'The Pilgrim’s Progress','John Bunyan','Allegory','lost-property','Christian leaves the City of Destruction with a burden on his back and walks through the Slough of Despond, Vanity Fair and the Valley of the Shadow of Death towards the Celestial City. Bunyan, a tinker and preacher, began it in Bedford gaol, and it was published in 1678. For two hundred years it stood in nearly every English home that had books at all.'],
  [376,'A Journal of the Plague Year','Daniel Defoe','History','lost-property','The Great Plague of London in 1665, told by a saddler who stayed in the city, reading the weekly bills of mortality and watching houses shut up with red crosses on their doors. Defoe was a small child at the time and wrote it in 1722. It reads like the account of a witness, which is the art of it.'],
  [9371,'The Praise of Folly','Desiderius Erasmus','Satire','lost-property','Folly herself makes the speech, in praise of the foolishness that makes life bearable and of all who serve her, scholars, monks, popes and princes above all. Erasmus wrote it in 1509 at Thomas More’s house in London, and its Latin title, Moriae Encomium, puns on his friend’s name. It was a bestseller, and it made him enemies.'],
  [1300,'Riders of the Purple Sage','Zane Grey','Western','lost-property','In southern Utah, a Mormon woman who owns a ranch refuses to marry the elder who demands her, and a black-clad gunman named Lassiter rides in looking for a grave. Zane Grey’s novel of 1912 became one of the best-selling Westerns ever written. Mind the canyon at the end.'],
  [246,'Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám','Omar Khayyám','Poetry','lost-property','Edward FitzGerald’s free English version of quatrains attributed to Omar Khayyám, the Persian astronomer and mathematician. It went almost unsold in 1859 until copies were found in a London bookseller’s penny box and passed round by Rossetti and his friends. “A Book of Verses underneath the Bough, A Jug of Wine, a Loaf of Bread—and Thou.”'],
  [804,'A Sentimental Journey Through France and Italy','Laurence Sterne','Travel','lost-property','Parson Yorick sets out across France meaning to describe it, and describes his own feelings instead, mostly about the women he meets. Sterne published it in 1768, weeks before he died, and it never reaches Italy. It is short, tender and full of dashes.'],
  [5720,'A Shropshire Lad','A. E. Housman','Poetry','lost-property','Sixty-three short poems of country lads, market towns, soldiering, hangings and early death, published at Housman’s own expense in 1896. Housman was a professor of Latin who had grown up in Worcestershire, looking west to the Shropshire hills. It sold slowly at first, then for decades.'],
  [217,'Sons and Lovers','D. H. Lawrence','Novel','lost-property','Paul Morel grows up in a Nottinghamshire mining village between a father who drinks and a mother who puts all her hopes in her sons. Lawrence’s novel of 1913 is close to his own life, and his mother died while he was writing it. Neither of the young women Paul loves stands a chance against her.'],
  [15396,'Tender Buttons','Gertrude Stein','Poetry','lost-property','Gertrude Stein’s book of 1914, in three parts, Objects, Food and Rooms, describes things by every word except the usual ones. The first piece is called “A Carafe, That Is a Blind Glass.” Readers have argued ever since about what it means, which may be the point.'],
  [15408,'Three Lives','Gertrude Stein','Stories','lost-property','Three women in a city called Bridgepoint: the good Anna and the gentle Lena, both German servants, and Melanctha, a young Black woman whose long story fills the middle of the book. Stein had it printed at her own expense in 1909. It is written in repetitions, the way people circle a thought.'],
  [2084,'The Way of All Flesh','Samuel Butler','Novel','lost-property','Four generations of the Pontifex family, and above all Ernest, crushed by a clergyman father and a pious mother and getting free of them very slowly. Samuel Butler, who also wrote Erewhon, worked on it for years and left it to be published after his death, in 1903. It takes the Victorian family apart from the inside.'],
  [416,'Winesburg, Ohio','Sherwood Anderson','Stories','lost-property','Linked stories of a small Ohio town, the lonely people Anderson calls grotesques, and George Willard, the young reporter they confide in. Sherwood Anderson published it in 1919. Hemingway and Faulkner both learned from him.'],
  [2527,'The Sorrows of Young Werther','Johann Wolfgang von Goethe','Novel','lost-property','Letters from a young man who falls hopelessly in love with Lotte, who is engaged to someone else. Goethe published it in 1774, when he was twenty-four, and was famous across Europe almost at once. Young men took to dressing like Werther, in a blue coat and a yellow waistcoat.'],
  [1845,'Zuleika Dobson','Max Beerbohm','Satire','lost-property','A conjuror’s assistant of fatal beauty comes to stay with her grandfather, the Warden of Judas College, and every undergraduate in Oxford falls in love with her. Max Beerbohm’s only novel, of 1911, follows the consequences with a perfectly straight face. Even the stone emperors’ heads outside the Sheldonian break into a sweat.'],
  [null,'The Peterkin Papers','Lucretia P. Hale','Humour','lost-property','Mrs Peterkin puts salt in her coffee instead of sugar, and the family tries the chemist and the herb-woman before the lady from Philadelphia suggests making a fresh cup. Lucretia P. Hale’s stories of a hopeless Boston family ran in magazines for years and became a book in 1880. Nearly every problem in the house is solved the same way.'],

  // ---------- The Unwelcome Spines (game.js, buildContestedRoom): banned, burned, prosecuted or suppressed ----------
  [28948,'The Rainbow','D. H. Lawrence','Contested','contested','Three generations of the Brangwen family on the borders of Nottinghamshire and Derbyshire, from the farm to the mining town, and Ursula, who wants more than any of them. Weeks after it was published in 1915 the police seized it, and a magistrate ordered more than a thousand copies destroyed as obscene. Lawrence’s publisher did not defend it.'],
  [4240,'Women in Love','D. H. Lawrence','Contested','contested','The Brangwen sisters, Ursula and Gudrun, and the two men they love, from the Midlands to the snows of the Tyrol. After the trouble over The Rainbow no English publisher would risk it, and it came out first in New York in 1920, sold by subscription. Its American publisher, Thomas Seltzer, was prosecuted for it and acquitted.'],
  [689,'The Kreutzer Sonata','Leo Tolstoy','Contested','contested','On a long train journey a man tells a stranger how he came to kill his wife, and why he blames marriage itself. Tolstoy’s story of 1889 was forbidden by the Russian censor, and in 1890 the United States Post Office refused to carry newspapers that printed it. Beethoven’s sonata, which sets things off, is innocent.'],
  [608,'Areopagitica','John Milton','Contested','contested','Milton’s speech to Parliament, printed in 1644, against the licensing of books before they could be published. “As good almost kill a man as kill a good book,” he wrote, and he printed it without a licence. It has been quoted in nearly every argument about censorship since.'],
  [3742,'Rights of Man','Thomas Paine','Contested','contested','Paine’s answer to Edmund Burke’s attack on the French Revolution, published in two parts in 1791 and 1792: governments exist for the people, and the people may change them. It sold in enormous numbers. Paine was tried for seditious libel and convicted in his absence, having already left for France.'],
  [3743,'The Age of Reason','Thomas Paine','Contested','contested','Paine’s case for believing in God without believing in the Bible, which he takes apart book by book. In England its printers and sellers were prosecuted for blasphemy for decades, and Richard Carlile went to prison for selling it. Paine finished the first part in Paris expecting to be arrested by the Revolution, and was.'],
  [18569,'Philosophical Dictionary','Voltaire','Contested','contested','Voltaire’s pocket dictionary of 1764, alphabetical and merciless, on superstition, fanaticism, tolerance and the follies of theologians. It was burned in Geneva and condemned in Paris, and he denied writing it. In 1766 a copy was burned at Abbeville with the body of the young Chevalier de La Barre, executed for blasphemy.'],
  [null,'Emile','Jean-Jacques Rousseau','Contested','contested','Rousseau’s treatise of 1762 on bringing up a child according to nature, the boy Emile learning from things rather than books. Its chapter on religion, the Creed of a Savoyard Priest, had it burned in Paris and in Geneva, and Rousseau fled to avoid arrest. It changed how Europe thought about childhood all the same.'],
  [989,'A Theologico-Political Treatise','Benedict de Spinoza','Contested','contested','Spinoza’s argument for reading the Bible as a book written by people in history, and for leaving thought free in a free state. He published it anonymously in 1670, under the name of an invented printer in Hamburg. It was banned in Holland four years later, and he was called an atheist for the rest of his life.'],
  [1200,'Gargantua and Pantagruel','François Rabelais','Contested','contested','The giant Gargantua, his son Pantagruel and their friend Panurge eat, drink, argue and go to sea, in five books of glorious excess. The theologians of the Sorbonne condemned it as obscene. Rabelais, a monk turned physician, first published it under an anagram of his name, Alcofribas Nasier.'],
  [null,'The Flowers of Evil','Charles Baudelaire','Contested','contested','Baudelaire’s poems of Paris, love, decay and boredom, published in 1857. Within weeks he and his publishers were prosecuted for offending public morals, fined, and made to cut six poems. A French court did not lift the ban on those six until 1949.'],
  [5250,'Nana','Émile Zola','Contested','contested','Nana, an actress with no talent and enormous success, ruins the rich men of the Second Empire one after another. Zola published it in 1880. In 1889 his English publisher, Henry Vizetelly, went to prison at sixty-nine for publishing Zola in English.'],
  [42704,'Salome','Oscar Wilde','Contested','contested','Wilde’s one-act play, written in French, about the princess who dances for Herod and asks for the head of John the Baptist. In 1892, with Sarah Bernhardt already in rehearsal, the Lord Chamberlain refused it a licence, because biblical characters could not be shown on the stage. It was first performed in Paris in 1896, while Wilde was in prison.'],
  [1097,'Mrs. Warren’s Profession','George Bernard Shaw','Contested','contested','A clever young Cambridge graduate discovers how her mother paid for her education: by running brothels across Europe. Shaw wrote it in 1893 and the Lord Chamberlain banned it, so for decades London could see it only in private performances. In New York in 1905 the police arrested the cast after the first night.'],
  [8121,'Ghosts','Henrik Ibsen','Contested','contested','Mrs Alving has spent her life hiding what her husband was, and builds an orphanage in his name; then her son comes home from Paris carrying his father’s inheritance. Ibsen’s play of 1881 was refused by the theatres of Scandinavia and first staged in Chicago, in Norwegian. A London newspaper called it “an open drain.”'],
  [8771,'Jurgen','James Branch Cabell','Contested','contested','A middle-aged pawnbroker is given back a year of his youth and spends it travelling through legend, Heaven and Hell, charming queens and goddesses on the way. In 1920 the New York Society for the Suppression of Vice had Cabell’s comedy seized for its double meanings. The case took two years to fail, and made the book famous.'],
  [233,'Sister Carrie','Theodore Dreiser','Contested','contested','A country girl comes to Chicago, finds factory work hard and men easy, and rises on the New York stage while the man who ruined himself for her goes down. Dreiser’s publisher, Doubleday, wished it had never signed for it in 1900, and printed it and barely sold it. It is now counted among the great American novels.'],
  [23700,'The Decameron','Giovanni Boccaccio','Contested','contested','Ten young Florentines escape the plague of 1348 to a villa in the hills and tell a hundred stories in ten days: lovers, tricksters, saints and lecherous friars. In 1559 it went on the Church’s first Index of forbidden books, and for years afterwards was printed only in purged editions. Chaucer and Shakespeare both borrowed from it.'],
  [null,'The Well of Loneliness','Radclyffe Hall','Contested','contested','Stephen Gordon, born into an English county family and named for the son her father wanted, grows up knowing she will love women. In 1928 a London magistrate condemned Radclyffe Hall’s novel as obscene and ordered the copies destroyed, though its boldest line is “and that night they were not divided.” A New York court cleared it the following year.'],
  [null,'All Quiet on the Western Front','Erich Maria Remarque','Contested','contested','Paul Bäumer and his classmates go straight from school to the trenches, and one by one do not come back. Remarque, who had fought in the war himself, published it in 1929, and it sold in millions around the world. In 1933 the Nazis burned it and banned it.'],
  [null,'A Farewell to Arms','Ernest Hemingway','Contested','contested','An American ambulance officer on the Italian front, the retreat from Caporetto, and an English nurse called Catherine Barkley. When it ran as a serial in 1929, the Boston police banned the issues of Scribner’s Magazine that carried it. In Mussolini’s Italy it was banned for its account of the retreat.'],
  [null,'Elmer Gantry','Sinclair Lewis','Contested','contested','A hard-drinking college football player discovers he has a gift for preaching, and rises through American religion without believing a word of it. Sinclair Lewis published it in 1927, and Boston banned it. It was denounced from pulpits across the country, which helped it sell.'],
  [3076,'Ten Days That Shook the World','John Reed','Contested','contested','John Reed’s eyewitness account of the Bolsheviks taking power in Petrograd in November 1917, published in 1919 with an introduction by Lenin. Under Stalin it was suppressed in the Soviet Union, because Trotsky is everywhere in it and Stalin hardly at all. Reed died in Moscow in 1920 and is buried by the Kremlin wall.'],
  [2162,'Anarchism and Other Essays','Emma Goldman','Contested','contested','Emma Goldman’s essays of 1910 on anarchism, prisons, patriotism, marriage and the traffic in women. She was jailed more than once for what she said in public, about birth control and against the draft among other things. In 1919 the United States deported her to Russia, which she found no freer.'],
  [null,'Walker’s Appeal','David Walker','Contested','contested','David Walker, a free Black clothes dealer in Boston, called in 1829 on enslaved Americans to resist, and on the country to read its own Declaration of Independence. Southern states made circulating it a crime, and Georgia offered a reward for him. He sent copies south with sailors, some sewn into clothing.'],
  [null,'The Necessity of Atheism','Percy Bysshe Shelley','Contested','contested','A short pamphlet of 1811 arguing that there is no proof that God exists, written by two Oxford undergraduates. One of them, Percy Bysshe Shelley, sent copies to bishops and to the heads of the colleges. He and his friend Thomas Jefferson Hogg were expelled within weeks.'],
  [null,'The Awakening of Spring','Frank Wedekind','Contested','contested','Teenagers in a small German town are told nothing about sex, and Wedekind’s play shows what their ignorance costs them. He wrote it in 1891, and it was not staged until 1906, in Berlin, and then with cuts. Its frankness has kept it in trouble ever since.'],
  [null,'The Impending Crisis of the South','Hinton Rowan Helper','Contested','contested','Hinton Rowan Helper, a white North Carolinian, argued in 1857 that slavery was ruining the poor white farmers of the South. Southern states banned it, and in North Carolina a minister was convicted for circulating it. Endorsing it cost John Sherman the Speakership of the House.'],
];
